ied in the\n  GateLedger and surfaced in the result at every loop exit; non-blocking\n  findings present at convergence are preserved as dissent (orca's\n  GateLedger, agent-arena's dissent preservation).\n- **Every loop is bounded** (constitution rule 8): ``max_rounds`` is\n  required and finite; exhaustion is its own typed outcome.\n\nExceptions from ``fix_fn`` propagate raw: a crashing fixer is an agent\nfailure for the caller's taxonomy to classify, never something this loop\nabsorbs into a fake verdict. ``verify_fn``/``review_fn``/``fingerprint_fn``\nfailures are treated as evidence-collection failures: the round cannot\nconverge, cannot count toward stalemate, and is recorded with a warning.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom collections.abc import Callable\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom enum import Enum\nfrom functools import partial\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom .canonical import hash_canonical\nfrom .git_evidence import capture_git_evidence\nfrom .verification import VerificationResult\n\n\nclass ReviewVerdict(str, Enum):\n    PASS = \"PASS\"\n    FAIL = \"FAIL\"\n    UNCERTAIN = \"UNCERTAIN\"\n\n\nclass Severity(str, Enum):\n    CRITICAL = \"CRITICAL\"\n    MAJOR = \"MAJOR\"\n    MINOR = \"MINOR\"\n    INFO = \"INFO\"\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Finding:\n    severity: Severity\n    category: str\n    description: str\n    reviewer: str\n    evidence: str = \"\"\n    file: str | None = None\n    symbol: str | None = None\n    confidence: float = 1.0\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"severity\": self.severity.value, \"category\": self.category,\n            \"description\": self.description, \"reviewer\": self.reviewer,\n            \"evidence\": self.evidence, \"file\": self.file, \"symbol\": self.symbol,\n            \"confidence\": self.confidence,\n        }\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ReviewReport:\n    \"\"\"One independent reviewer's structured verdict for one round.\"\"\"\n\n    verdict: ReviewVerdict\n    findings: tuple[Finding, ...] = ()\n    reviewer: str = \"unknown\"\n    notes: str = \"\"\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass FixRequest:\n    round_index: int\n    blocking_findings: tuple[Finding, ...]\n    verification: VerificationResult | None\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass FixReport:\n    \"\"\"The fixer's own account of its round. ``claims_done`` is a SIGNAL,\n    never evidence â€” the next round's verification/review decide.\"\"\"\n\n    claims_done: bool = False\n    cannot_fix: bool = False\n    notes: str = \"\"\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass GatedFinding:\n    finding: Finding\n    gate_reason: str\n    round_index: int\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"finding\": self.finding.to_dict(),\n            \"gate_reason\": self.gate_reason,\n            \"round_index\": self.round_index,\n        }\n\n\nclass ConvergenceOutcome(str, Enum):\n    CONVERGED = \"CONVERGED\"\n    STALEMATE = \"STALEMATE\"\n    CANNOT_FIX = \"CANNOT_FIX\"\n    ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED = \"ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED\"\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass RoundRecord:\n    index: int\n    fingerprint: str | None\n    evidence_ok: bool\n    verification: VerificationResult | None\n    review: ReviewReport | None\n    blocking: tuple[Finding, ...]\n    gated: tuple[GatedFinding, ...]\n    fix: FixReport | None\n    warnings: tuple[str, ...]\n    unchanged_streak: int\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ConvergenceResult:\n    outcome: ConvergenceOutcome\n    rounds: tuple[RoundRecord, ...]\n    gate_ledger: tuple[GatedFinding, ...]\n    dissent: tuple[Finding, ...]\n    warnings: tuple[str, ...]\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"outcome\": self.outcome.value,\n            \"round_count\": len(self.rounds),\n            \"gate_ledger\": [g.to_dict() for g in self.gate_ledger],\n            \"dissent\": [f.to_dict() for f in self.dissent],\n            \"warnings\": list(self.warnings),\n        }\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ConvergencePolicy:\n    max_rounds: int\n    max_unchanged_rounds: int = 3\n    blocking_severities: frozenset[Severity] = frozenset({Severity.CRITICAL, Severity.MAJOR})\n    min_blocking_confidence: float = 0.5\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        if self.max_rounds < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"ConvergencePolicy.max_rounds must be >= 1\")\n        if self.max_unchanged_rounds < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"ConvergencePolicy.max_unchanged_rounds must be >= 1\")\n        if not 0.0 <= self.min_blocking_confidence <= 1.0:\n            raise ValueError(\"min_blocking_confidence must be within [0, 1]\")\n\n\ndef git_fingerprint(repo_path: Path) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"Repo-state fingerprint for stalemate detection: HEAD + working-tree\n    status + diff stat, hashed with the kernel's canonical contract.\n    Returns None when evidence collection fails (not a repo, no HEAD), so\n    the caller knows not to count the round.\"\"\"\n    evidence = capture_git_evidence(repo_path)\n    if not evidence.is_repo or evidence.head_sha is None:\n        return None\n    return hash_canonical({\n        \"head\": evidence.head_sha,\n        \"status\": evidence.status_porcelain,\n        \"diff\": evidence.diff_stat,\n    })\n\n\nclass ConvergenceLoop:\n    \"\"\"Bounded implementâ†’verifyâ†’reviewâ†’fix convergence engine.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        policy: ConvergencePolicy,\n        verify_fn: Callable[[], VerificationResult],\n        review_fn: Callable[[int], ReviewReport],\n        fix_fn: Callable[[FixRequest], FixReport],\n        fingerprint_fn: Callable[[], str | None],\n    ):\n        self.policy = policy\n        self.verify_fn = verify_fn\n        self.review_fn = review_fn\n        self.fix_fn = fix_fn\n        self.fingerprint_fn = fingerprint_fn\n\n    def run(self) -> ConvergenceResult:\n        rounds: list[RoundRecord] = []\n        ledger: list[GatedFinding] = []\n        loop_warnings: list[str] = []\n        prev_fingerprint: str | None = None\n        have_prev = False\n        unchanged = 0\n        last_fix: FixReport | None = None\n\n        for index in range(1, self.policy.max_rounds + 1):\n            warnings: list[str] = []\n\n            fingerprint = self._collect(self.fingerprint_fn, \"fingerprint\", warnings)\n            verification = self._collect(self.verify_fn, \"verification\", warnings)\n            review = self._collect(partial(self.review_fn, index), \"review\", warnings)\n            evidence_ok = (\n                fingerprint is not None and verification is not None and review is not None\n            )\n\n            # Signal âˆ§ evidence: a prior DONE claim with an unchanged repo\n            # is downgraded to a warning, never trusted.\n            if (\n                last_fix is not None and last_fix.claims_done\n                and fingerprint is not None and have_prev\n                and fingerprint == prev_fingerprint\n            ):\n                warnings.append(\n                    \"fixer claimed done but the repo fingerprint did not \"\n                    \"change: signal without evidence, continuing\"\n                )\n\n            # Stalemate accounting â€” only rounds with a real fingerprint\n            # count; the counter resets on any observed change.\n            if fingerprint is not None:\n                if have_prev and fingerprint == prev_fingerprint:\n                    unchanged += 1\n                else:\n                    unchanged = 0\n                prev_fingerprint = fingerprint\n                have_prev = True\n\n            blocking: list[Finding] = []\n            gated_this_round: list[GatedFinding] = []\n            if review is not None:\n                for finding in review.findings:\n                    if finding.severity not in self.policy.blocking_severities:\n                        gated_this_round.append(GatedFinding(\n                            finding, f\"severity {finding.severity.value} below blocking set\", index,\n                        ))\n                    elif finding.confidence < self.policy.min_blocking_confidence:\n                        gated_this_round.append(GatedFinding(\n                            finding,\n                            f\"confidence {finding.confidence} below floor \"\n                            f\"{self.policy.min_blocking_confidence}\",\n                            index,\n                        ))\n                    else:\n                        blocking.append(finding)\n                ledger.extend(gated_this_round)\n\n            clean = (\n                evidence_ok\n                and verification is not None and verification.passed\n                and review is not None and review.verdict is ReviewVerdict.PASS\n                and not blocking\n            )\n\n            fix: FixReport | None = None\n            if not clean and evidence_ok:\n                needs_fix = bool(blocking) or (\n                    verification is not None and not verification.passed\n                )\n                if needs_fix:\n                    fix = self.fix_fn(FixRequest(\n                        round_index=index,\n                        blocking_findings=tuple(blocking),\n                        verification=verification,\n                    ))\n                    last_fix = fix\n                else:\n                    # e.g. an UNCERTAIN verdict with nothing concrete to\n                    # fix: another independent round decides; a reviewer\n                    # who stays uncertain over an unchanging repo runs\n                    # into the stalemate breaker, not an infinite loop.\n                    warnings.append(\n                        \"round not clean but no fixable target (verdict \"\n                        f\"{review.verdict.value if review else 'n/a'}): \"\n                        \"scheduling another independent round\"\n                    )\n\n            loop_warnings.extend(warnings)\n            rounds.append(RoundRecord(\n                index=index, fingerprint=fingerprint, evidence_ok=evidence_ok,\n                verification=verification, review=review,\n                blocking=tuple(blocking), gated=tuple(gated_this_round),\n                fix=fix, warnings=tuple(warnings), unchanged_streak=unchanged,\n            ))\n\n            if clean:\n                dissent = tuple(\n                    f for f in (review.findings if review else ())\n                    if f not in blocking\n                )\n                return self._result(\n                    ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED, rounds, ledger, dissent, loop_warnings,\n                )\n            if fix is not None and fix.cannot_fix:\n                return self._result(\n                    ConvergenceOutcome.CANNOT_FIX, rounds, ledger, (), loop_warnings,\n                )\n            if unchanged >= self.policy.max_unchanged_rounds:\n                return self._result(\n                    ConvergenceOutcome.STALEMATE, rounds, ledger, (), loop_warnings,\n                )\n\n        return self._result(\n            ConvergenceOutcome.ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED, rounds, ledger, (), loop_warnings,\n        )\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _collect(fn: Callable[[], Any], label: str, warnings: list[str]) -> Any:\n        \"\"\"Evidence collection: a failing probe yields None + a warning,\n        never a fake value and never an aborted loop.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - probe failure is data, not control flow\n            warnings.append(f\"{label} collection failed: {exc}\")\n            return None\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _result(\n        outcome: ConvergenceOutcome,\n        rounds: list[RoundRecord],\n        ledger: list[GatedFinding],\n        dissent: tuple[Finding, ...],\n        warnings: list[str],\n    ) -> ConvergenceResult:\n        # Nothing-lost rule: the gate ledger rides on EVERY exit path.\n        return ConvergenceResult(\n            outcome=outcome, rounds=tuple(rounds), gate_ledger=tuple(ledger),\n            dissent=dissent, warnings=tuple(warnings),\n        )\n\r\nimport subprocess\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom gnosis.kernel.convergence import (\n    ConvergenceLoop,\n    ConvergenceOutcome,\n    ConvergencePolicy,\n    Finding,\n    FixReport,\n    FixRequest,\n    ReviewReport,\n    ReviewVerdict,\n    Severity,\n    git_fingerprint,\n)\nfrom gnosis.kernel.verification import VerificationResult\n\n\ndef _verification(passed: bool) -> VerificationResult:\n    return VerificationResult(\n        name=\"fake\", passed=passed, exit_code=0 if passed else 1,\n        duration_s=0.01, stdout_excerpt=\"\", stderr_excerpt=\"\",\n    )\n\n\ndef _finding(severity: Severity = Severity.MAJOR, confidence: float = 1.0,\n             description: str = \"bug\") -> Finding:\n    return Finding(severity=severity, category=\"correctness\",\n                   description=description, reviewer=\"fake-reviewer\",\n                   confidence=confidence)\n\n\nclass _Script:\n    \"\"\"Deterministic per-round script for verify/review/fingerprint/fix.\n    Each stream keeps its own call counter (they are each called exactly\n    once per round, in loop order fingerprint -> verify -> review).\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, verifications, reviews, fingerprints):\n        self._verifications = list(verifications)\n        self._reviews = list(reviews)\n        self._fingerprints = list(fingerprints)\n        self.fix_requests: list[FixRequest] = []\n        self.fix_reports: list[FixReport] = []\n        self._calls = {\"verify\": 0, \"review\": 0, \"fingerprint\": 0}\n\n    def _take(self, stream: str, seq):\n        self._calls[stream] += 1\n        item = seq[min(self._calls[stream], len(seq)) - 1]\n        if isinstance(item, Exception):\n            raise item\n        return item\n\n    def verify(self):\n        return self._take(\"verify\", self._verifications)\n\n    def review(self, round_index: int):\n        return self._take(\"review\", self._reviews)\n\n    def fingerprint(self):\n        return self._take(\"fingerprint\", self._fingerprints)\n\n    def fix(self, request: FixRequest) -> FixReport:\n        self.fix_requests.append(request)\n        report = (self.fix_reports[len(self.fix_requests) - 1]\n                  if len(self.fix_reports) >= len(self.fix_requests)\n                  else FixReport(claims_done=True))\n        return report\n\n\ndef _run(script: _Script, policy: ConvergencePolicy | None = None):\n    loop = ConvergenceLoop(\n        policy or ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=10, max_unchanged_rounds=3),\n        verify_fn=script.verify, review_fn=script.review,\n        fix_fn=script.fix, fingerprint_fn=script.fingerprint,\n    )\n    return loop.run()\n\n\nclass TestConvergenceLoop(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_clean_first_round_converges(self):\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS)],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.rounds), 1)\n        self.assertEqual(script.fix_requests, [])\n\n    def test_findings_then_fix_then_converges(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True), _verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,)),\n                     ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS)],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\", \"fp2\"],  # the fix changed the repo\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.rounds), 2)\n        self.assertEqual(script.fix_requests[0].blocking_findings, (bad,))\n\n    def test_verification_failure_blocks_convergence_despite_pass_verdict(self):\n        # Evidence beats signal: a PASS review cannot converge a failing\n        # verification.\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(False), _verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS),\n                     ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS)],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\", \"fp2\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.rounds), 2)\n        # The fixer was pointed at the verification failure.\n        self.assertEqual(len(script.fix_requests), 1)\n        self.assertFalse(script.fix_requests[0].verification.passed)\n\n    def test_cannot_fix_is_a_typed_exit_with_ledger(self):\n        gated = _finding(Severity.MINOR, description=\"style nit\")\n        blocking = _finding(Severity.CRITICAL)\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(blocking, gated))],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\"],\n        )\n        script.fix_reports = [FixReport(cannot_fix=True, notes=\"beyond me\")]\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CANNOT_FIX)\n        # Nothing lost: the gated finding rides out on the failure exit.\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.gate_ledger), 1)\n        self.assertIs(result.gate_ledger[0].finding, gated)\n\n    def test_stalemate_after_n_unchanged_fingerprints(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 10,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))] * 10,\n            fingerprints=[\"same\"] * 10,  # fixes never change the repo\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.STALEMATE)\n        # Round 1 sets the baseline; three consecutive unchanged rounds\n        # (2, 3, 4) trip the breaker.\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.rounds), 4)\n        self.assertEqual(result.rounds[-1].unchanged_streak, 3)\n\n    def test_fingerprint_change_resets_the_stalemate_counter(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 6,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))] * 6,\n            fingerprints=[\"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"b\", \"b\", \"b\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script, ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=6, max_unchanged_rounds=3))\n        # Streak reached 2 at round 3, reset by \"b\" at round 4, reached 2\n        # again at round 6: the loop exhausts instead of stalemating.\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED)\n        streaks = [r.unchanged_streak for r in result.rounds]\n        self.assertEqual(streaks, [0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2])\n\n    def test_failed_fingerprint_collection_never_counts_toward_stalemate(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 6,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))] * 6,\n            fingerprints=[\"same\", None, None, None, None, \"same\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script, ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=6, max_unchanged_rounds=3))\n        # Four of the six rounds had no usable fingerprint: only rounds 1\n        # and 6 count, giving a streak of 1 â€” no STALEMATE.\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED)\n        self.assertEqual(result.rounds[-1].unchanged_streak, 1)\n\n    def test_rounds_exhausted_is_bounded_and_typed(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 3,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))] * 3,\n            fingerprints=[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"] * 1,\n        )\n        result = _run(script, ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=3, max_unchanged_rounds=99))\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED)\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.rounds), 3)\n\n    def test_low_confidence_blocking_finding_is_gated_not_dropped(self):\n        shaky = _finding(Severity.CRITICAL, confidence=0.2, description=\"maybe?\")\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS, findings=(shaky,))],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)  # gated out\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.gate_ledger), 1)\n        self.assertIn(\"confidence\", result.gate_ledger[0].gate_reason)\n\n    def test_minor_findings_do_not_block_but_survive_as_dissent(self):\n        nit = _finding(Severity.MINOR, description=\"naming nit\")\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS, findings=(nit,))],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)\n        self.assertIn(nit, result.dissent)\n        self.assertEqual(len(result.gate_ledger), 1)  # and in the ledger\n\n    def test_done_claim_without_repo_change_is_downgraded_to_warning(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 3,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))] * 3,\n            fingerprints=[\"same\"] * 3,\n        )\n        script.fix_reports = [FixReport(claims_done=True)] * 3\n        result = _run(script, ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=3, max_unchanged_rounds=99))\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.ROUNDS_EXHAUSTED)\n        self.assertTrue(any(\"signal without evidence\" in w for w in result.warnings))\n\n    def test_uncertain_verdict_never_converges_and_stalemates_out(self):\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)] * 10,\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.UNCERTAIN)] * 10,\n            fingerprints=[\"same\"] * 10,\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.STALEMATE)\n        self.assertEqual(script.fix_requests, [])  # nothing concrete to fix\n\n    def test_probe_exception_is_a_warning_not_a_crash_or_a_stalemate_count(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        boom = RuntimeError(\"probe exploded\")\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[boom, _verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS),\n                     ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.PASS)],\n            fingerprints=[boom, \"fp2\"],\n        )\n        result = _run(script)\n        self.assertEqual(result.outcome, ConvergenceOutcome.CONVERGED)\n        self.assertFalse(result.rounds[0].evidence_ok)\n        self.assertTrue(any(\"verification collection failed\" in w for w in result.warnings))\n        self.assertEqual(result.rounds[1].unchanged_streak, 0)\n        del bad\n\n    def test_fixer_exception_propagates_raw(self):\n        bad = _finding()\n        script = _Script(\n            verifications=[_verification(True)],\n            reviews=[ReviewReport(verdict=ReviewVerdict.FAIL, findings=(bad,))],\n            fingerprints=[\"fp1\"],\n        )\n\n        def crashing_fix(request: FixRequest) -> FixReport:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"fixer crashed\")\n\n        loop = ConvergenceLoop(\n            ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=3),\n            verify_fn=script.verify, review_fn=script.review,\n            fix_fn=crashing_fix, fingerprint_fn=script.fingerprint,\n        )\n        with self.assertRaises(RuntimeError):\n            loop.run()\n\n    def test_policy_validation(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n            ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=0)\n        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n            ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=1, max_unchanged_rounds=0)\n        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n            ConvergencePolicy(max_rounds=1, min_blocking_confidence=1.5)\n\n\nclass TestGitFingerprint(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fingerprint_tracks_repo_changes_and_fails_closed(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n            repo = Path(tmp) / \"repo\"\n            repo.mkdir()\n            self.assertIsNone(git_fingerprint(repo))  # not a repo yet\n            subprocess.run([\"git\", \"init\"], cwd=repo, check=True, capture_output=True)\n            self.assertIsNone(git_fingerprint(repo))  # repo but no HEAD yet\n            subprocess.run([\"git\", \"config\", \"user.email\", \"e@x.com\"], cwd=repo, check=True)\n            subprocess.run([\"git\", \"config\", \"user.name\", \"T\"], cwd=repo, check=True)\n            (repo / \"a.txt\").write_text(\"one\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n            subprocess.run([\"git\", \"add\", \"-A\"], cwd=repo, check=True, capture_output=True)\n            subprocess.run([\"git\", \"commit\", \"-m\", \"init\"], cwd=repo,\n                           check=True, capture_output=True)\n            first = git_fingerprint(repo)\n            self.assertIsNotNone(first)\n            self.assertEqual(git_fingerprint(repo), first)  # stable when unchanged\n            (repo / \"a.txt\").write_text(\"two\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n            self.assertNotEqual(git_fingerprint(repo), first)  # dirty tree differs\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n\r\n\"\"\"Git evidence capture.\n\nRead-only: this module never mutates the target repository. It snapshots\ngit status, the current HEAD, branch, and a diff stat so a run's effect\non the tree is deterministically recorded evidence rather than an LLM's\nclaim about what it changed.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom datetime import UTC, datetime\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\n_SHA_RE = re.compile(r\"[0-9a-f]{40,64}\\Z\")\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass GitEvidence:\n    is_repo: bool\n    head_sha: str | None\n    branch: str | None\n    status_porcelain: str\n    diff_stat: str\n    ts: str = field(default_factory=lambda: datetime.now(UTC).isoformat())\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"is_repo\": self.is_repo, \"head_sha\": self.head_sha, \"branch\": self.branch,\n            \"status_porcelain\": self.status_porcelain, \"diff_stat\": self.diff_stat, \"ts\": self.ts,\n        }\n\n\ndef _run_git(repo_path: Path, args: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str]:\n    try:\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [\"git\", *args], cwd=str(repo_path), capture_output=True, text=True,\n            timeout=30, check=False,  # rc is returned and handled by callers\n        )\n        return proc.returncode, (proc.stdout or proc.stderr).strip()\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        return 127, \"git executable not found\"\n\n\ndef capture_git_evidence(repo_path: Path) -> GitEvidence:\n    code, _ = _run_git(repo_path, [\"rev-parse\", \"--is-inside-work-tree\"])\n    if code != 0:\n        return GitEvidence(is_repo=False, head_sha=None, branch=None, status_porcelain=\"\", diff_stat=\"\")\n\n    _, head_sha = _run_git(repo_path, [\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"])\n    _, branch = _run_git(repo_path, [\"rev-parse\", \"--abbrev-ref\", \"HEAD\"])\n    _, status = _run_git(repo_path, [\"status\", \"--porcelain\"])\n    _, diff_stat = _run_git(repo_path, [\"diff\", \"--stat\"])\n\n    return GitEvidence(\n        is_repo=True,\n        # `git rev-parse HEAD` on a repo with no commits echoes the literal\n        # string \"HEAD\" to stdout (the fatal goes to stderr), so a prefix\n        # check alone recorded a bogus head. Only a real sha counts.\n        head_sha=head_sha if _SHA_RE.fullmatch(head_sha) else None,\n        branch=branch,\n        status_porcelain=status,\n        diff_stat=diff_stat,\n    )\n\r\n7----\n8-\n9-## Summary table\n10-\n11-| Repo | Tier focus | Risk | Decision | Signature mechanism |\n12-|---|---|---|---|---|\n13-| bernstein | Kernel authority, journaling, replay | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Hash-chained journals + hermetic replay + signed lineage, all on one canonical-JSON primitive |\n14-| orca | Workflow-as-code orchestration | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Typed resumable stages where each stage = one git commit + progress-log checkpoint |\n15-| agent-workspace-fabric | Worktree lifecycle control plane | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Frozen transition allow-list + pure `decide()` monitor core + fencing-epoch claims |\n16-| cezar | Multi-runner CLI orchestration | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Evidence-graded usage-limit parsing with durable auto-resume and a two-hold account model |\n17:| ralphex | Review convergence loops | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Dual-evidence completion gate + git-fingerprint stalemate counter |\n18-| smithers | Durable time-travel runtime | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Write-ahead tool journal + leased/fenced destructive rewind in one transaction |\n19-| beads | Persistent task graph / work queue | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Materialized `is_blocked` + row-version CAS claims + ephemeral lease plane |\n20-| agent-governance-toolkit | Policy decision engine | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Stateless fail-closed PDP: snapshot → one verdict, every error path denies |\n21-| grit | Semantic (AST-symbol) ownership | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Atomic symbol leases with dependency-aware transitive read-locking |\n22-| opentraces | Trace/evidence capture | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Content-addressed hash-chained event log in git refs + closed evidence-tier vocabularies |\n23-| nono | OS-level sandbox | MEDIUM | DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE | Landlock + seccomp-notify kernel-enforced deny-by-default |\n24-| agentjail | Tool-call guardrail | MEDIUM | ADAPT_IDEA | Parsed command-intent → Rego allow/ask/deny with rule_id provenance |\n25-| sandbox-probe | Sandbox boundary verification | LOW | DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE | Boundary = measured diff between host baseline and in-sandbox probe reports |\n26-| reprise | Deterministic record/replay | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Occurrence-aware content-hash replay from a single SQLite trace |\n27-| agent-capsule | Signed session receipts | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Six-question capsules, SHA3-256 chained + Ed25519-signed, with per-action authority field |\n28-| catacomb | Statistical regression gate | LOW | DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE | Redact-before-persist evidence dirs + A/A-calibrated statistical gate |\n29-| spec-kit | Spec-driven phase gating | LOW | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Phase-gated command templates with typed handoffs and per-phase artifacts |\n30-| superpowers | Enforced process skills | MEDIUM | ADAPT_IDEA | Meta-skill forcing process-skill invocation before any action |\n31-| agent-arena | Heterogeneous multi-agent debate | LOW | ADAPT_IDEA | 12-mode escalation ladder + blind judging + mandatory dissent preservation |\n32-| TwinRouterBench | Per-step model routing benchmark | MEDIUM | BENCHMARK_AGAINST | Downgrade-search \"cheapest sufficient tier\" labels for prefix-conditioned routing |\n33-| symphony | Orchestration state machine spec | MEDIUM | REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN | Single-mutation-authority orchestrator with reconcile-before-dispatch |\n34-| dagger | Content-addressed execution DAG | MEDIUM | ADAPT_IDEA | Recursive call-digest identity → deterministic memoized execution |\n35-| opentelemetry-collector | Telemetry pipeline semantics | LOW | ADAPT_IDEA | Per-consumer MutatesData declarations + reverse-topological startup |\n36-\n37----\n38-\n39-## Architecture directives for the GNOSIS kernel\n40-\n41:The strongest cross-repo convergences, stated as implementable kernel rules. Each directive is evidenced by multiple independent codebases that arrived at the same mechanism.\n42-\n43-### 1. One canonical-bytes hash primitive under every chain\n44-\n45-**Evidence:** bernstein (journal/WAL/work ledger/lineage share one `canonical_json` contract), opentraces (event_id = sha256 of canonical JSON including sequence + previous pointer), agent-capsule (canonical JSON → SHA3-256 → signed), reprise (`canonical_bytes`/`blob_hash`), agent-governance-toolkit (action identity = sha256 over key-sorted canonical JSON), dagger (recursive call digests).\n46-\n47-**Rule:** The kernel defines exactly one primitive — `canonical_json = dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\",\":\")).encode(\"utf-8\")` — and every hash in the system (journal entries, event ids, replay keys, action identities, approval bindings) derives from it. Document the contract so a third party can re-derive any hash by hand. Bernstein's warning applies: chain proliferation without a shared contract (five parallel event systems) is the failure mode to design out from day one.\n48-\n49-### 2. Append-only, hash-chained journal with fail-closed recovery\n50-\n51-**Evidence:** bernstein (`Journal._validate_chain_for_recovery` recomputes every hash from genesis, never trusts the tail), opentraces (CAS append to a git ref with bounded retry; content-addressed ids make replay order tamper-evident), agent-workspace-fabric (`WorkspaceEvent` append-only, per-workspace sequence counter, \"no UPDATE/DELETE ever\"), cezar (per-run NDJSON append + atomic tmp+rename index).\n52-\n53-**Rule:** Kernel events are append-only rows with a per-scope monotonic sequence and a previous-hash pointer. On open, recompute the whole chain: a torn final line is a legitimate crash (keep the prefix); any interior corruption, sequence gap, or prev-hash break refuses to open and names the offending line. Never extend a chain from an unverified anchor.\n54-\n55-### 3. State machine as a single frozen allow-list, with gates projected from it\n56-\n57-**Evidence:** agent-workspace-fabric (`MappingProxyType` of `frozenset` transitions + `assert_transition` typed errors + documented salvage edges), symphony (orchestrator is the *only* component that mutates scheduling state; SPEC §7 vocabulary), bernstein (`approval_gate.py` derives approvable/completable sets from `tasks/lifecycle.py` constants so no transport gets weaker rules), spec-kit (phase-gated pipeline with typed handoffs), cezar (review park is a store-owned state, not a prompt convention).\n58-\n59-**Rule:** Allowed Task transitions live once, in one importable kernel module, as immutable data. Every surface (CLI, MCP, HTTP, adapter) imports them; illegal transitions raise a typed error carrying `(from, to, allowed_next)`. The kernel is the *sole writer* of status — structurally, not by convention (AWF's guard-by-convention gap is the anti-pattern). Include explicit salvage edges for restart recovery, and gate spec→plan→tasks→implement phases as first-class states so no Task reaches implementation without a versioned spec artifact upstream.\n60-\n61-### 4. Two-plane ownership: durable claims + ephemeral fenced leases\n62-\n63-**Evidence:** beads (claim = CAS on a `row_lock` version token, in the same transaction that computed readiness; leases/heartbeats in a non-replicated ephemeral table; TTL reclaim with recovery events), agent-workspace-fabric (fencing epochs bumped as a SQL expression so stale workers are fenced on their next CAS), smithers (durable rewind lease with `checkStillHeld` re-checked *inside* the transaction), grit (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` atomic check-then-set symbol leases — plus the fencing hole GNOSIS must close), cezar (repo-root promise-chain lease; account-scoped holds).\n64-\n65-**Rule:** Separate history-worthy ownership transitions (durable, versioned) from liveness chatter (ephemeral leases + heartbeats that never enter history). Claims are conditional CAS on a version token rewritten by every ownership mutation, idempotent for same-actor retries, returning typed conflict data (holder, status) — never prose. Fencing epochs increment monotonically inside the claim transaction; expired leases are reclaimed by TTL sweep that repeats the expiry predicate at delete time. A lease stays bound to unresolved integration state — never release it just because a merge failed (grit's mistake).\n66-\n67-### 5. Worktree lifecycle with provenance-gated destruction\n68-\n69-**Evidence:** agent-workspace-fabric (per-repo bare mirror + per-workspace worktree, idempotent removal, cleanup that deliberately avoids blanket `reset --hard`), cezar (idempotent worktree creation, never deletes non-empty unregistered dirs, autosave refuses mid-merge/conflict-marker states and tags its reason), orca (`BranchMode.Created` gate — only branches orca minted may be auto-deleted; resume header validated as hostile input before any destructive git action), grit (merge under PID-liveness-checked lock, preserves branch+worktree on failure), bernstein (content-addressed snapshots under refs, fork refuses on sha mismatch).\n70-\n71-**Rule:** The kernel mints per-task worktrees/branches and records that provenance at creation time; destruction is gated on \"did the kernel mint this\". Creation is idempotent with reattach paths; non-empty unregistered directories are recoverable work, never deleted. Autosave commits carry a reason tag and refuse conflict-marker states — losing a recovery point beats poisoning the branch. Any resume/recovery header is validated as hostile input (ref-shape checks, protected-branch floor, cross-checks) before any destructive operation; invalid state is a hard abort, and corrupt-state is distinguished from absent-state.\n72-\n73:### 6. Review convergence: signal ∧ evidence, tri-state rounds, stalemate fingerprints, nothing-lost ledger\n74-\n75:**Evidence:** ralphex (ALL_TASKS_DONE discarded if unchecked boxes remain; DONE means \"zero issues this round\", not \"I finished fixing\"; git HEAD+diff fingerprint stalemate counter), orca (bounded review loop with hard caps, per-severity confidence gates, and a `GateLedger` where every gated-out or declined finding is carried and printed at exit), cezar (review park state the agent cannot bypass; done-signals parsed only from the agent's own turn text), agent-arena (independent generation → evidence checking → blind judging, dissent preserved in the synthesis artifact).\n76-\n77:**Rule:** An agent's DONE claim never closes a Task. The kernel re-checks a durable artifact (plan checkboxes, git HEAD/diff fingerprint) and downgrades signal-without-evidence to continue-with-warning. Rounds are tri-state: clean round → converged; fixed-something → schedule another independent verification round; can't-fix → typed failure. A stalemate counter over repo-state fingerprints (N consecutive unchanged rounds → `STALEMATE`) is a kernel circuit breaker, resets on change, and never counts when evidence collection itself failed. Every finding held back by a gate is carried in a ledger and surfaced at every loop exit — the loop cannot silently drop evidence. Dissent from independent reviewers is preserved in the evidence artifact.\n78-\n79-### 7. Strict replay as default; write-ahead intent before side effects\n80-\n81-**Evidence:** bernstein (`DeterministicStore` — replay miss aborts, never silently falls through to a live model; per-key FIFO preserves call order and multiplicity; response-determining params folded into the key), reprise (occurrence-aware Nth-identical-request matching from a content-addressed SQLite blob store; strict-mode `ReplayMiss`), smithers (journal row with status `intended` + {sideEffect, idempotent, hasRevert, idempotencyKey} persisted *before* any tool executes; rewind safety becomes a decidable query), dagger (same recipe → same digest → memoized result), catacomb (recorded evidence as regression fixtures).\n82-\n83-**Rule:** Record every LLM/tool call keyed by a content hash of its response-determining parameters (model, provider, temperature, max_tokens, effort). Replay is occurrence-aware and strict: a miss is an abort; live fall-through is an explicit opt-in. Before any side-effecting tool executes, persist an `intended` journal row with side-effect provenance flags, so rewind/fork/compensation safety is answered by querying the journal, never by guessing. Golden runs become regression fixtures.\n84-\n85-### 8. Fail-closed, stateless policy verdicts; honesty about enforcement\n86-\n87-**Evidence:** agent-governance-toolkit (ACS: complete JSON snapshot in → exactly one of allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform out; *every* runtime failure collapses to deny with a reserved `runtime_error:*` reason policies may not emit; unconfigured intervention point denies; undeclared tool denies; approvals bound to the exact action hash), agentjail (verdicts over parsed command intent — program/flags/resolved paths — with rule_id provenance), nono (kernel-enforced deny-by-default as the floor beneath advisory hooks), orca (enforcement honesty matrix: every backend restriction classified Hard/SandboxApprox/PromptOnly/Ignored, machine-checked in tests), opentraces (single three-way egress predicate; `unknown` is never coerced to safe).\n88-\n89-**Rule:** The kernel's Policy Engine is a pure, stateless function: adapters build a complete snapshot per intervention point; the engine returns exactly one verdict; every internal failure returns deny with a reserved reason namespace, so a deny-by-error is always distinguishable from a policy deny. Missing bindings and undeclared tools fail closed — configuration gaps are never consent. NEEDS_HUMAN escalations bind approval to the sha256 action identity of exactly what was evaluated (anti-TOCTOU). Decisions key on structured, parsed intent — never string pattern-matching with hardcoded bypass lists. And per adapter, maintain a machine-checked enforcement matrix recording whether each restriction is mechanically enforced, sandbox-approximated, prompt-only, or ignored — the kernel's least-privilege decisions consume honest data.\n90-\n91-### 9. Typed failure taxonomy end-to-end; rate limits as park states; recovery as reconcile\n92-\n93-**Evidence:** cezar (evidence-ordered usage-limit parsing that records *which* evidence shape carried each classification and refuses to guess; durable `autoResumeAt`; deadline holds vs in-flight holds keyed by runner:credential; boot re-adoption that revives through the normal queue path or fails loudly), smithers (`waiting-quota` durable park state with `resetAtMs`; startup crash recovery from pre-mutation audit rows), ralphex (three-tier retry/limit/error taxonomy with signal-suppressed retries), agent-workspace-fabric (reason codes flowing exception → log → append-only event → failure taxonomy → scheduler policy, with a generated catalog as the drift gate), symphony (distinct terminal reasons Failed/TimedOut/Stalled/Canceled because \"retry logic and logs differ\"; reconcile-before-dispatch on every poll tick), beads (typed `ClaimConflictError` carrying holder+status).\n94-\n95-**Rule:** Failures are typed at the adapter boundary (structured exit/frame codes first; prose parsing only as a last-resort enricher, with the evidence grade recorded on every classification). Reason codes flow unchanged from exception to append-only event to scheduler policy — implementing GNOSIS rules 5–7 (`RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE`; `FAIL_INFRA` doesn't penalize the agent). `RATE_LIMITED` is a durable park state carrying a reset timestamp, with account-scoped holds distinguishing \"window known shut — block everything on this credential\" from \"one resume is probing — block only new work\". Recovery is reconcile, not restore: durable state on the record, volatile timers rebuilt idempotently on every scheduler pump, plus a boot sweep that re-adopts every stranded run through normal paths or fails it loudly — never leaving a ghost.\n96-\n97----\n98-\n99-## Contradictions and open questions\n100-\n101-1. **Fail-open vs fail-closed evidence sealing.** Bernstein's completion evidence gate is deliberately fail-open (\"Evidence sealing must NEVER block… any error is caught and swallowed\") and agent-capsule's adapters are fail-open by design; ACS, opentraces' egress predicate, and GNOSIS Rule 2 demand fail-closed. The tension is availability (don't lose finished work over a sealing bug) vs integrity (no DONE without evidence). GNOSIS's constitution settles the default (fail closed), but the open question is the recovery path: what evidence would settle the cost is a chaos test measuring, under injected sealing failures, the rate of lost-but-recoverable work in a fail-closed design vs the rate of falsely-closed Tasks in a fail-open one.\n102-\n103:2. **Loop exhaustion semantics.** Ralphex soft-continues on max-iterations (\"continuing…\") and reads unchanged HEAD as convergence even when the reviewer may simply have failed to act; orca hard-caps with a nothing-lost ledger; AWF's PR monitor has *no* iteration/wall-clock terminal gate at all, delegating livelock to human-notification gates (in tension with GNOSIS Rule 8). Evidence to settle: measure, on a replayed corpus of review loops, how often exhaustion-with-flow-through ships unconverged defects vs how often hard-stop + NEEDS_HUMAN merely delays a run that would have converged.\n104-\n105-3. **Advisory vs enforced ownership.** Grit's symbol locks are pure convention (`cmd_done` commits everything touched and never diffs changes against held locks); orca enforces \"fan-out can read, never commit\" at compile time via capability tokens; the deep analyses converge on a kernel merge gate (diff hunks mapped to AST symbols, `FAIL_POLICY` if changed-symbols ⊄ claimed-symbols) that no surveyed repo actually implements. Evidence to settle: a multi-agent conflict-injection benchmark counting how many unclaimed-symbol mutations reach merge under advisory-only vs gate-enforced ownership, and the gate's false-positive rate on legitimate refactors.\n106-\n107-4. **Where failure classification lives.** Cezar and ralphex classify failures by regex over provider prose at the terminal error; AWF, ACS, and symphony type failures structurally at the boundary. Cezar's evidence-graded prose parsing is the best of its kind, but it is inherently provider-version-fragile. Evidence to settle: a per-backend audit of what fraction of real failure events carry structured codes (exit codes, JSON frames) vs prose-only — if structured coverage is high, prose parsing is demoted to enricher; where it is low, cezar's evidence-ordered recognizers are the fallback.\n108-\n109:5. **In-band signals vs kernel-owned state.** Ralphex matches `<<<RALPHEX:...>>>` anywhere in output (a model *quoting* the marker triggers it, defended only by prompt exhortation); cezar narrows to line-anchored markers parsed only from the agent's own turn text, never tool output; cezar's review gate goes further — the state is store-owned and no marker can bypass it. Evidence to settle: marker-quoting injection tests against each scheme; the survey already strongly suggests kernel-owned out-of-band state wins, with in-band markers acceptable only as hints that never directly transition state.\n110-\n111-6. **Destructive teardown discipline.** Orca performs `git reset --hard` + gated `clean` in failure teardown (safe only because its recovery header is validated as hostile first); cezar and AWF deliberately refuse blanket resets, preferring autosave commits and idempotent reattach. GNOSIS Rule 27 forbids reset-as-shortcut, but orca shows a disciplined, validated reset can make resume-in-place trivial. Evidence to settle: crash-injection runs comparing recovery-loss incidents (work destroyed by teardown) vs poisoned-workspace incidents (stale state corrupting the resumed run) under each discipline.\n112-\n113-7. **Storage substrate weight vs scale.** Beads (Dolt, 60+ migrations, proxy daemon) and AWF (Postgres + Compose stacks) carry heavy substrates for semantics that cezar, reprise, grit, and smithers deliver on SQLite/plain files; beads' claim scan is also deliberately unbounded over the ready set (acknowledged in claim.go). SQLite-V1 is the GNOSIS ADR baseline. Evidence to settle: a scheduler benchmark at GNOSIS's actual scale envelope (hundreds to low-thousands of Tasks) measuring whether the materialized-`is_blocked` + CAS-claim pattern on SQLite holds up before any heavier substrate is even discussed.\n114-\n115-8. **Rate-limit hold granularity.** Cezar's two-hold account model (deadline holds block everything on a credential; in-flight holds block only new work — each kind fixed a real bug in opposite directions) vs smithers' simpler single `waiting-quota` park. Evidence to settle: reproduce resume-stampede and resume-deadlock scenarios with multiple concurrent runs sharing one credential; if both reproduce, the two-hold model is required, not optional.\n116-\n117----\n118-\n119-## Per-repository findings\n120-\n121-*Deep analyses (10 repositories), followed by group sweeps (3 blocks). Sections are reproduced verbatim from the archaeology agents, evidence labels preserved.*\n122-\n123-### bernstein — deterministic multi-CLI-agent orchestrator with hash-chained journals, signed lineage, and hermetic replay; the strongest kernel-authority reference found for GNOSIS\n124-\n125-- **Identity:** https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein @ 7dce402 (2026-08-18) | Apache-2.0, standard text, no riders (verbatim tail: \"Copyright 2026 Alex Chernysh\"; separate NOTICE + TRADEMARKS.md) | Python >=3.12 (dev-pinned 3.14), ~1,879 .py files / ~721k LOC in src, repo 114 MB | hatchling + uv (uv.lock; no custom hatch build hooks — VERIFIED via pyproject grep) | runtime: Click CLI, FastAPI task server, Textual TUI, MCP server, spawns 40+ external coding-agent CLIs\n126-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code is defensively written and local-first (servers default-bind 127.0.0.1 and refuse non-loopback without a token, `mcp/remote_transport.py:309` VERIFIED; telemetry default-off with DO_NOT_TRACK precedence, SELF-REPORTED docstring invariants; docker.sock mount commented out VERIFIED), but it is a ~720k-LOC system that spawns agent subprocesses by design, has 3 `pickle.loads` sites on local files (`cli/commands/cache_cmd.py:331`, `persistence/fingerprint.py:286`, `planning/duration_predictor.py:285` VERIFIED), and `benchmark/programbench.py:453-463` `exec()`s candidate code in-process (VERIFIED). Fine as READ-ONLY reference; unacceptable as a runtime dependency surface.\n127-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — Bernstein independently converged on GNOSIS's exact constitution (kernel owns state/gates, agents propose, evidence before consensus) and implements the deepest journal/gate/replay machinery I have seen, with genuine Windows lanes (msvcrt lock paths, `newline=\"\"` byte-exact appends, PowerShell rmtree fallback, windows CI + `.github/windows-lane-baseline.json`). But adopting it as a dependency would import a monolith that violates GNOSIS Rule 30 (simplicity is a security property); the load-bearing mechanisms are each a few hundred lines of stdlib-only Python and their contracts are documented well enough to re-derive by hand. Reimplement the chain/replay/gate contracts natively in the GNOSIS kernel; keep bernstein as the audit oracle for design decisions.\n128-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n129-  - Hash-chained step journal with fail-closed recovery (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/journal.py:Journal`, `_validate_chain_for_recovery`): each step hashed as SHA256(canonical compact sorted-keys JSON of {prev_hash, input_hash, model, prompt, tool_call, tool_result, effort?}); on open the tip is recovered by **recomputing every hash from genesis, never trusting the tail**; a torn final line degrades gracefully but any interior corruption / seq gap / prev_hash break raises `JournalError` so a tampered chain cannot be extended from a poisoned anchor. Schema evolution by omission: `effort=None` keys byte-identically to legacy rows, so old chains re-verify unchanged. [VERIFIED — read full file]\n130-  - Hermetic deterministic replay (`src/bernstein/core/orchestration/deterministic.py:DeterministicStore`, `ReplayMissError`, `ReplayRecordingMissingError`; plus `replay/journal.py:EventJournal`): every LLM call recorded keyed by SHA256(model\\0prompt\\0provider\\0temperature\\0max_tokens); replay keeps a **per-key FIFO preserving call order and multiplicity**; a miss (including over-consumption) **aborts by default** instead of silently falling through to a live model, and whole-run replay refuses up front when no recording exists; live fall-through is an explicit env opt-in. `EventJournal` Merkle-chains all run events with **wall-clock excluded from the payload hash**, so byte-identical executions yield identical chains and divergence surfaces at a precise step index. [VERIFIED — read both files]\n131-  - Portable work ledger anchored in git refs (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/work_ledger.py`, `ledger_git.py`): the task graph and every transition form a hash chain (same canonical-JSON contract as the journal); **redaction runs before hashing** so the portable chain verifies anywhere without carrying cleartext; anchored under `refs/bernstein/work-ledger/<run-id>` as a **timestamp-free deterministic tree** (two operators anchoring the same chain get byte-identical trees); resume appends a nonce-bearing `run.resumed` entry so double-resume becomes structurally divergent and is caught at the next anchor, never silently merged. [VERIFIED — read module docstrings + hashing contract; internals NOT TESTED]\n132-  - Signed lineage transparency log + read-only CI gate (`src/bernstein/core/lineage/gate.py:check`): per-entry Ed25519 detached JWS verified against agent cards keyed by **(agent_id, kid)** (kid-substitution and key-rotation safe), optional operator HMAC, steward allowlist for merge entries, dangling-parent and unresolved-fork detection; verification binds to **exact on-disk bytes** (`canonicalise(entry) == raw_line`, strict `b\"\\n\"` splitting) to defeat re-canonicalisation and newline-reframing attacks. The gate is a pure function over a frozen log + cards dir — the reviewer never mutates what it judges. [VERIFIED — read full file]\n133-  - Decision WAL + gates projected from one state machine (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/wal.py:WALEntry` fsync-per-entry hash-chained decisions with `committed` flag and a rebuildable uncommitted-index cache; `src/bernstein/core/orchestration/phase_gates.py` deterministic stdlib rule runner R001–R005 with exactly-one bounded auto-repair re-fire; `src/bernstein/mcp/approval_gate.py` derives approvable/completable status sets **from `tasks/lifecycle.py` constants so no transport can obtain a weaker rule**). [VERIFIED — read wal.py head, phase_gates.py head, approval_gate.py head]\n134-  - Fork-from-step on git snapshots (`src/bernstein/core/replay/fork.py`): each step's snapshot is a content-addressed git commit under `refs/bernstein/snapshots/<run_id>/<N>`; forking cross-verifies the journal-recorded sha against the ref and **refuses on mismatch**, and the child run's first journal event parent-links the fork point, turning the run chain into a verifiable tree. [VERIFIED — read module docstring/flow; execution NOT TESTED]\n135-- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) The single canonical-bytes contract — one `canonical_json = dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\",\":\")).encode(\"utf-8\")` primitive shared by journal, WAL, work ledger and lineage, documented so a third party can re-derive any hash by hand; make it the GNOSIS Kernel's one chain primitive. (2) Fail-closed tail recovery semantics: recompute the whole chain on open, distinguish \"torn final line = legitimate crash, keep prefix\" from \"interior corruption = refuse and name the line\". (3) Strict-replay-as-default: a replay miss is an abort (`ReplayMissError`), never a silent live call, with response-determining params (model/provider/temperature/max_tokens/effort) folded into the replay key so parameter drift can't masquerade as a hit. (4) Gate authority projected from the state machine: allowed transitions live once in the task-lifecycle module and every surface (CLI/MCP/HTTP) imports them, so no entry point gets weaker rules; plus git-ref anchoring of the ledger with timestamp-free trees for portable, deterministic resume.\n136-- **What to avoid:** (1) The completion evidence gate is deliberately **fail-open** (`evidence/completion_gate.py`: \"Evidence sealing must NEVER block… any error is caught and swallowed\") — that directly contradicts GNOSIS Rule 2 (no DONE without evidence); GNOSIS's completion gate must fail closed. (2) Chain proliferation and monolith accretion: five historical event systems later needing a \"unifying projection\" (`events/feed.py` admits this), a 6,785-line orchestrator.py, and parallel hash-chain implementations (journal, WAL, EventJournal, work ledger, lineage, audit chain) that only partially share one contract — plus `pickle` for local caches where JSON would do.\n137-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 9, security 8, recovery 9, auditability 10, modularity 6, testability 9 (Hypothesis stateful WAL-recovery machine, lineage tamper-detection integration tests, mutation testing wired for lineage — VERIFIED test tree), windows 8, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 9 (as pattern source; ~2 as dependency)\n138-\n139-### orca — Workflow-as-code agent orchestration: typed, resumable Scala flows where deterministic structure (stages+git) owns nondeterministic agent operations\n140-\n141-- **Identity:** https://github.com/VirtusLab/orca @ 9da3d07 (2026-08-09) | Apache-2.0, standard text, no riders (VERIFIED — read head+tail of LICENSE) | Scala 3.8.4 (~75k LOC, 449 files) + POSIX sh | sbt (multi-module: tools/flow/runner/shell + 5 backend drivers); flows are `scala-cli` `.sc` scripts, JVM 21 | ~1.4 MiB pack\n142-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no telemetry found, MCP/HTTP listeners are loopback-only (`McpHost.scala`: Netty on `127.0.0.1`, ephemeral port, VERIFIED); but by design it runs repo-committed lint/format commands via `bash -c` (`Lint.scala:33`, settings from `.orca/settings.properties`), performs `git reset --hard` + `git clean -fdq` in failure teardown (`GitTool.scala:646-655`), spawns vendor agent CLIs as subprocesses, and `install.sh` is curl|bash that bootstraps scala-cli remotely and ships a shim resolving unpinned `latest.release` at every launch (all VERIFIED). Fine as read-only source; unaudited execution not advised.\n143-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — orca is the strongest workflow-as-code specimen in the registry for GNOSIS's exact thesis: a deterministic host program (typed stages, git commits, bounded loops) owns state and gates while LLM agents only propose within them. But it is Scala/JVM with an explicit \"Windows beyond the bash -c contract is a non-goal\" (`docs/research/shell/06-wizard-settings.md`, VERIFIED), and its capability enforcement leans on Scala 3 capture checking — untransplantable as a dependency. Reimplement its stage/progress-log/recovery patterns in the GNOSIS Python kernel.\n144-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n145-  - Resumable checkpointed stages (`flow/src/main/scala/orca/Flow.scala:stage`, `StageFrames.scala`): `stage(name){body}` gets a hierarchical id `parent/name#occurrence` from a frame stack of per-parent occurrence counters; a fresh run serializes the body's typed result JSON into the progress log and makes ONE git commit covering code+log; on re-run, a log entry that decodes to `T` replays the result and skips the body (decode failure = fail-safe re-run). Invariants: exactly-once occurrence bump per attempt (stable ids across resumes); skipped parents structurally can't desync nested counters; owner-thread assert rejects `stage()` from forks. Failure mode: outer stage re-running over a nested stage's committed partial work requires idempotent bodies (documented verbatim in scaladoc). [VERIFIED]\n146:  - Durable progress log + untrusted-on-load validation (`flow/.../progress/ProgressStore.scala`, `RecoveryCheck.scala`): prompt-hash-keyed JSON file written via temp-file + atomic rename (torn-write proof), tri-state load (`Absent`/`Corrupt`/`Loaded`) so corrupt≠absent gets a loud warning; on resume the header is treated as hostile input — branch-ref shape checks blocking CLI-flag injection/path traversal (`isSafeReusedRef`), protected-branch floor (`main`/`master` + detected default), promptHash recompute, and a current-branch cross-check — all before any destructive git action; invalid = hard abort, never silent fresh start. [VERIFIED]\n147-  - Lifecycle recovery with disjoint teardowns (`runner/.../FlowLifecycle.scala`): setup refuses to start a fresh run on a branch another run's log already claims (`abortIfBranchBusy`); failure teardown `reset --hard` (+ gated clean) back to the last stage commit so re-run resumes in place; success teardown removes the log in a final commit and auto-deletes only branches orca itself minted (`BranchMode.Created` gate defeats tampered-header-driven deletion); deterministic `flow-<hash>` fallback branch names so unattended runs never flip on naming collisions. Plus dual locks (`FlowLock.scala`): process AtomicBoolean + PID lockfile with CREATE_NEW-only atomicity, stale-lock steal by delete+re-race, bounded at 4 attempts. [VERIFIED]\n148-  - Capability-token effect discipline (`flow/.../FlowControl.scala`, `InStage`/`WorkspaceWrite` tokens, `Session.scala:OutsideStage`): who may start stages (`FlowControl`), mutate the workspace (`WorkspaceWrite`), or mint durable sessions (compile-time proof of being *outside* a stage via `NotGiven[InStage]`) is encoded in types; forks receive only the narrow read+emit `FlowContext`, enforced by Scala capture/separation checking in `ReviewLoop.scala` plus a runtime owner-thread assert as backstop — \"LLM fan-out can read, never commit\" is a compile error, not a convention. [VERIFIED]\n149:  - Bounded review-and-fix loop with a nothing-is-lost ledger (`flow/.../review/ReviewLoop.scala`, `ConfidenceGate.scala`, `GateLedger.scala`): parallel reviewers with durable per-reviewer sessions re-reviewing only the delta since `lastSent`; per-severity confidence bars (tentative Critical admitted at 0.5, uncertain Info gated at 0.8); hard caps (3 fix attempts) and a halt when the fixer reports zero fixes; every finding gated out, declined, or unaccounted-for is returned in `IgnoredIssues` with the last-known reason and printed at exit — the loop cannot silently drop evidence. Fixer's declines (not its \"fixed\" claims) are fed to next-round reviewers, so reviewers re-derive fixes independently. [VERIFIED]\n150-  - Enforcement honesty matrix (`tools/.../agents/Enforcement.scala` + `EnforcementTableTest`): every backend's actual enforcement of a tool restriction is classified `Hard`/`SandboxApprox`/`PromptOnly`/`Ignored` with per-cell rationale, machine-checked in tests — the framework refuses to pretend a prompt-level \"read-only\" is a sandbox. [VERIFIED]\n151-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n152-  - **Stage = typed checkpoint = one git commit.** Persist each stage's typed result JSON in a run-scoped progress log, commit code+log atomically, and resume by replaying decoded results with decode-failure→re-run as the fail-safe; use hierarchical `name#occurrence` ids minted by a frame stack so same-named and nested stages stay stable across resumes.\n153-  - **Treat your own recovery state as hostile input.** Validate resume headers (ref-shape/injection checks, protected-branch floor, prompt-hash, current-branch cross-check) before any destructive operation; make invalid state a hard abort, distinguish corrupt-log from absent-log, and gate branch deletion on \"did I mint this branch\" recorded at creation time.\n154:  - **Nothing-lost gate ledger for review loops.** Every finding a gate holds back or a fixer declines is carried, deduped by title with the freshest reason, and returned+printed at every loop exit — maps directly onto GNOSIS's EVIDENCE BEFORE CONSENSUS and incident→test rules.\n155-  - **Enforcement matrix as first-class policy metadata.** For each adapter (Claude/Codex), record whether a permission restriction is mechanically enforced, sandbox-approximated, prompt-only, or ignored — and machine-check the table in tests, feeding the Kernel's least-privilege decisions with honest data.\n156-- **What to avoid:** (1) `bash -c` execution of commands read from a repo-committed settings file that an agent itself auto-discovers and writes — a prompt-injected agent can plant a shell command a later run executes; GNOSIS's Policy Engine must admission-check any repo-sourced command. (2) The unpinned `latest.release` launcher shim (every launch resolves the newest published artifact) — violates GNOSIS reproducibility; pin versions in provenance. Also note the documented resume hazard: outer-stage bodies must be idempotent over their own committed leftovers — design stages so edits never straddle a nested stage.\n157-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 8, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 8, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 3, op-simplicity 5, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\n158-\n159-### agent-workspace-fabric — control plane that runs AI coding agents through isolated worktree→validate→PR→monitor→merge lifecycles\n160-\n161-- **Identity:** https://github.com/dimileeh/agent-workspace-fabric @ 45693fe | Apache-2.0, no riders (standard text verbatim, appendix unfilled) [VERIFIED] | Python 3.12 (FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2 async, Pydantic v2, Alembic, Docker SDK) + TypeScript (Next.js console in `apps/console`) | uv/pyproject + npm | ~41 MB w/o `.git`, 484 src py files, 1030 test files, coverage gate `fail_under = 99` in pyproject [VERIFIED]\n162-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no telemetry, no eval/exec, only SafeLoader-subclass YAML, secrets redaction + egress audit modules present [VERIFIED]; but the control-plane containers run as root with CAP_SYS_ADMIN for overlayfs auth mounts (`docker/compose/local-service.yml:198`, `src/awf/node/auth_mounts_caps.py`), opt-in `privileged: true` DinD sidecar (`node/compose_manager.py:1131`), and it shells out to agent CLIs and git/docker pervasively (by design). `git push --force-with-lease` appears only in rebase recovery on AWF's own feature branch (`executor/git_methods.py:989`); worktree cleanup deliberately avoids blanket `reset --hard` (`executor/git_ops.py:337`) [VERIFIED]. Nothing runs at import/install time; pre-commit hooks are local lint/type checks [VERIFIED].\n163-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — this is the closest existing system to GNOSIS's task→worktree→isolated-run→validate→PR→integrate loop, and its three or four core patterns (immutable transition allow-list, pure no-I/O decision core, DB leases with fencing epochs, reason-codes flowing end-to-end into an append-only event log) map directly onto \"kernel owns state/gates, agents propose, evidence before consensus\". As a dependency it is rejected: Postgres + per-workspace Compose stacks + root/CAP_SYS_ADMIN worker + GitHub-as-source-of-truth is far too heavy and Linux-container-bound for a Windows-native SQLite-first kernel. Re-implement the mechanisms; benchmark GNOSIS's lifecycle vocabulary against its state diagram.\n164-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n165-  - Workspace state machine (`src/awf/control/state_machine.py:WorkspaceStateMachine`): a module-level `MappingProxyType` of `frozenset` allowed transitions (`requested→provisioning→ready→running→validating→pushing→monitoring_pr→completed`, plus non-terminal `blocked`/`recovering` pause states and `destroying→destroyed`); every status mutation must call `assert_transition(src, dst)` which raises a typed error carrying both endpoints for structured 409s. Invariant: no illegal transition can be silently written; terminal/callback-terminal sets gate cleanup and stale async callbacks. Failure mode: the guard is a convention (explicit call, not enforced by type system) — a forgotten call bypasses it. [VERIFIED]\n166-  - Pure PR-monitor decision core (`src/awf/runtime/pr_monitor.py:decide`): zero-I/O function `(PRStatus, MonitorState, MonitorConfig) → exactly one MonitorAction` over an explicitly ordered 10-gate table (merged/closed short-circuit → base-retarget drift → SyncBase-before-comments → operator hint → unresolved comments → blocking reviews → CI fail → CI pending → conflict → absent-required-checks grace → blocked/hooks → Merge-or-NotifyHuman when `auto_merge=False`); the runner (`runtime/pr_monitor_runner/runner.py`) does all I/O and persists `MonitorState`. Invariant: one action per call, gate order documented with the production incidents that forced it; no iteration/wall-clock terminal gate — exit only via Merge/ShortCircuit/Abort. Failure mode: livelock is possible by design and delegated to human notification gates. [VERIFIED]\n167-  - DB-backed claims with fencing epochs (`src/awf/control/worker/manager.py:ControlWorker`, `worker/claims.py:_apply_execution_claim`): worker polls Postgres with `SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, claims rows via conditional status CAS, stamps `execution_claimed_by` + expiry + `execution_claim_epoch = epoch + 1` as a SQL expression so a later claimant always lands a strictly higher epoch and a stale worker's heartbeat/release CAS is fenced. Invariant: at most one live driver per workspace across concurrent workers/restarts. Failure mode: epoch fencing covers only the provisioning path (executor path documented as epoch-unguarded). [VERIFIED]\n168-  - Mirror + worktree provisioner (`src/awf/node/git_manager.py:GitManager`): per-repo bare mirror (`clone --mirror`, then strips `remote.origin.mirror` so worktrees can push), per-workspace `git worktree add -b <branch> <tracking_ref>`; all mirror-registry mutations serialized by per-mirror asyncio locks keyed by event loop (worktree registry is a shared file that corrupts under concurrent add/prune — observed in their CI); removal is idempotent + `worktree prune`, and `_reclaim_stale_worktree` raises a reason-coded error on OSError rather than silently leaking disk. Invariant: base lookups go through remote-tracking refs so prune never touches AWF-created branches. [VERIFIED]\n169-  - Stale-execution recovery & GC (`src/awf/control/worker/recovery_stale.py`, `src/awf/service/gc*.py` — 15 modules): periodic scans classify runtime snapshots of stranded active workspaces and choose salvage (preserved-active resume, hosted PR re-adoption, remonitor of an open PR with a running stack) before failing; state machine carries dedicated salvage edges (e.g. `provisioning→monitoring_pr`, `validating→running` rewind). Invariant: cleanup is honest (failures recorded as events, never swallowed); orphan dirs reconciled against DB. [VERIFIED at structure level; individual salvage paths NOT TESTED]\n170-  - Reason codes + append-only evidence (`src/awf/db/models.py:WorkspaceEvent`, `common/reason_codes`-generated `docs/REASON_CATALOG.md` ~936 lines): exception `reason_code` → structured log → append-only `WorkspaceEvent` (per-workspace `event_sequence` counter, \"no UPDATE/DELETE ever\") → `FailureReason` → policy; `idempotency_key` unique constraints on workspaces/tasks/operations/callbacks; bounded validate/fix loop with explicit pass budgets (`executor/execution_validation.py: max_validation_attempts = max_fix_passes + conformance_budget + 1`) and flaky/setup retry budgets in `runtime/validation_runner.py`. [VERIFIED]\n171-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n172-  - The state machine as an importable, frozen allow-list module with `assert_transition` raising a typed error carrying `(from, to, allowed_next)` — plus documented salvage edges for restart recovery — as the Kernel's single status authority.\n173-  - The pure-`decide()` pattern for any monitor/review loop: snapshot + persisted state in, exactly one action out, all I/O in a thin runner — this makes GNOSIS gate policy unit-testable and replayable with hand-rolled snapshots.\n174-  - Claim leases with monotonic fencing epochs bumped as a DB expression (`epoch = epoch + 1` in the claim txn), so a superseded agent/worker is fenced on its next CAS — directly portable to SQLite.\n175-  - Reason-code plumbing end-to-end (exception → log field → append-only event → failure taxonomy → scheduler policy, e.g. `RETRY_BONUS_INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE` in `service/scheduler.py`) with a generated catalog doc as the drift gate — matches GNOSIS rules 5–7 exactly.\n176-- **What to avoid:** the `*DelegatesMixin` file-explosion style (~45 executor modules of free functions taking `self: Any`) trades away static typing and makes control flow archaeology hard; and the guard-by-convention state machine (nothing structurally prevents `workspace.status = ...` — GNOSIS should make the kernel the only writer of status, not a convention). Also avoid the root+CAP_SYS_ADMIN control-plane posture; keep GNOSIS least-privilege.\n177-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 9, security 6, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 3, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\n178-\n179-### cezar — local multi-runner (Claude/Codex/OpenCode/pi) coding-agent orchestrator with per-task worktrees, crash re-adoption and usage-limit auto-resume\n180-\n181-- **Identity:** https://github.com/open-mercato/cezar @ 9a8b24d (shallow clone, depth 1; HEAD 2026-08-19) | MIT, standard 21-line text, no riders (VERIFIED) | TypeScript on Node >= 20, npm workspaces, plain `tsc` build, Hono HTTP server; deps: hono, zod, ws, yaml, smol-toml, @clack/prompts | ~110k LoC TS across 4 packages, 17 MiB pack, 249 test files vs 256 source files\n182-- **Risk:** LOW (as read-only pattern source) — no install hooks (root and package `package.json` have no pre/postinstall; prebuild/postbuild are local build scripts) [VERIFIED]; server binds 127.0.0.1 by default with an anchored host-guard against DNS rebinding (`server/server.ts:~1224-1260`) [VERIFIED]; secret redaction before persisting transcripts (`core/secret-redaction.ts`, value+pattern, shared regex with child-env stripping) [VERIFIED]; destructive git ops scoped to `.ai/cezar/worktrees/<runId>` [VERIFIED]. Caveats if ever *run*: the zero-config tool default grants unrestricted `Bash` in cwd (self-documented as #430 in `agent-runner.ts`) and autosaves commit with `--no-verify`; `server-install` ships VPS/ngrok exposure scripts (opt-in, not audited in depth) [VERIFIED/NOT TESTED].\n183-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — This is the closest production-grade match yet to GNOSIS's priority problem (one seam over Claude Code and Codex CLIs with resume, worktrees, and gates), but it is a Node/TS application with a 3.6k-line manager class, not a library GNOSIS (Python/SQLite kernel) can depend on. Its mechanisms map one-to-one onto GNOSIS rules — `RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE` is literally implemented as evidence-ordered usage-limit parsing with durable resume deadlines, and the review park is a kernel-owned gate the agent cannot bypass. Reimplement the seam, the classification, and the recovery sweep in the GNOSIS kernel; benchmark nothing, depend on nothing.\n184-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n185-  - Runner abstraction (`packages/cezar/src/core/agent-runner.ts:AgentRunner/AgentSession/RUNNER_IDS`): one normalized `AgentEvent` stream + a persistent-process session (sendMessage/end/interrupt/result) implemented by 4 backends — claude = stream-json over stdin, codex = `codex app-server` JSON-RPC/JSONL (`thread/start`, `thread/resume`, `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`), opencode = HTTP+SSE, pi = JSONL RPC. `RUNNER_IDS` tuple is the single source of truth all schemas derive from; per-backend system-prompt mapping is explicit (claude `--append-system-prompt`, others prepend to first user message). Invariant: an exit cezar caused is never an agent failure — `isSignalTerminationExit(130/137/143)` paired with a \"we sent the signal\" flag, and `trackChildExit` deliberately avoids the `ChildProcess.killed` trap (#844). [VERIFIED]\n186-  - Failure classification + auto-resume (`core/usage-limit.ts:parseUsageLimit`, `workflows/run.ts:scheduleAutoResumeIfLimited/fireAutoResume/reconcileAutoResumes`): terminal error strings are classified in strict evidence order — Claude's machine marker `usage limit reached|<epoch>`, ISO timestamp in prose, wall-clock+timezone, relative delay — and each hit records which shape carried it (`evidence` field, quoted in the transcript). No reset instant ⇒ `null`, deliberately refusing to guess. Resume deadline is durable on the record (`autoResumeAt`); timers are process-local and rebuilt by a reconcile sweep on every pump, bounded by `MAX_AUTO_RESUMES=12`, a 24 h missed-deadline expiry, and a 30 s grace past the provider's reset. Account-scoped holds (`runner:profile` key) distinguish deadline holds (block everything — prevents resume stampede) from in-flight holds (block only new work — prevents resume deadlock). [VERIFIED]\n187-  - Crash recovery (`workflows/run.ts:recover()/reviveQueuedRun`, `handoff.ts`): on boot, `queued` runs are re-queued from the persisted `workflowDef` (or failed loudly — \"a run neither running nor failed is the worst failure this engine has\"); `waiting` runs settle like a closed session; `running` runs are marked `failed: interrupted` then immediately resumed via the last persisted `sessionId` with a prompt pointing the agent at its per-run handoff file. The engine itself heartbeats that handoff journal, so resume context exists even when the agent never wrote. A 60 s queue watchdog re-adopts records the live process lost. [VERIFIED]\n188-  - Worktree lifecycle (`git-worktree.ts:createWorktree/autosaveCommit/resolveBaseRef`; `workflows/run.ts:acquireRepoRoot`): per-run branch `cez/<id8>` under `.ai/cezar/worktrees/<runId>`; creation is idempotent with reattach paths and never deletes non-empty unregistered dirs (recoverable work). Autosave commits carry a reason tag (`periodic|turn end|run finalize|pre-PR`) and *refuse* to commit mid-merge or conflict-marker states (ordered `<<<<<<</=======/>>>>>>>` triple scan) — losing a recovery point beats poisoning the branch. Stale-base resolution prefers `origin/<base>` when local is behind, killing phantom inflated diffs. Runs in the repo root are serialized by a promise-chain lease held for the run's whole lifetime, whose waiters park and return their concurrency slot. [VERIFIED]\n189-  - Review gate (`runs/review-gate.ts:reviewGateEnabled` + `run.ts:settleSuccess`): a successful run parks at status `review` (blocking retention/cleanup) only when `worktreeHasDiff && gateEnabled && !autonomous` — a state the store owns, not a prompt convention; done-signals from agents are line-anchored in-band markers (`CEZ:DONE`, `CEZ:PR=`) parsed from the agent's own turn text only, never from tool output, so reading the contract cannot poison the record (`runs/task-markers.ts`). [VERIFIED]\n190-  - Durable store (`runs/store.ts`): `runs.json` index written atomic tmp+rename, per-run NDJSON event log via append; `reconcileLoadedRun` demotes impossible statuses at load time (a `running` record loaded without `keepLive` becomes `failed`). Plain files, no DB. [VERIFIED]\n191-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n192-  1. The two-hold account model for rate limits: a *deadline* hold (window known shut — blocks all work on that `runner:account` key, including other resumes) vs an *in-flight* hold (one resume is probing — blocks new work only). Each hold kind fixed a real bug in opposite directions; GNOSIS's Kernel should key limits by backend+credential, exactly this way.\n193-  2. Evidence-graded limit parsing that refuses to guess: ordered recognizers (machine marker > timestamp > clock > delay), a recorded `evidence` tag on every classification, `null` when no reset instant — surfaced honestly instead of retry-looping. Slots directly under GNOSIS's `RATE_LIMITED` taxonomy entry.\n194-  3. Recovery as reconcile, not restore: durable deadline/state on the record, volatile timers rebuilt idempotently on every scheduler pump plus a wedge watchdog, and boot re-adoption that either revives a run through the normal queue path or fails it loudly — never leaves a ghost.\n195-  4. Engine-side handoff journal per task (seeded idempotently, heartbeated by the kernel, injected via env var into every resume prompt) — resume context that survives the agent, the process, and the provider window.\n196-  5. Autosave that refuses conflict states and tags its reason — provenance-grade recovery commits without poisoning the branch.\n197-- **What to avoid:** (1) `RunManager` is a 3,645-line class owning queue, leases, resume, review, retention and prompt composition — GNOSIS must keep these as separate kernel modules with typed interfaces. (2) Failure classification exists *only* as regex over provider prose at the terminal error; GNOSIS should type failures at the adapter boundary (structured exit/frame codes first) and use prose parsing solely as a last-resort enricher — and never copy the default-unrestricted-Bash tool allowlist.\n198-- **Scores:** determinism 6, durability 8, security 6, recovery 9, auditability 8, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 7, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 8, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\n199-\n200-### ralphex — autonomous plan-execution loop (task → multi-agent review → external adversarial review → finalize) driving Claude/Codex CLIs\n201-\n202-- **Identity:** https://github.com/umputun/ralphex @ 935cc87 | MIT, standard text, no riders (VERIFIED) | Go 1.26 (+POSIX shell/Python wrapper scripts) | Makefile + go build, vendored deps (26MB) | ~17.3k LOC source Go, ~36.2k LOC tests, shallow clone (1 commit)\n203-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code itself is clean (no telemetry, dashboard binds 127.0.0.1 by default `pkg/web/server.go:41`, strips `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`/`CLAUDECODE` from child env `claudeChildEnv`, no `reset --hard`/`clean`/push, only `worktree remove --force` on its own worktrees — all VERIFIED), but its operating mode is intrinsically privileged: embedded default `claude_args = --dangerously-skip-permissions` (VERIFIED `pkg/config/defaults/config:23`) plus config-driven arbitrary script execution (`custom_review_script`, `vcs_command`, custom notify script). Docker-only opt-in `npm install @latest` at container start is documented, not in the binary.\n204:- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the convergence/stop-condition set (dual-evidence completion, tri-state review signal contract, git-fingerprint stalemate counter, three-tier retry/limit/error taxonomy) is the best worked-out implementation-review-rework loop seen so far and maps almost one-to-one onto GNOSIS rules 5–9/11. But ralphex is a prompt-governed CLI orchestrator where the LLM self-reports state via text markers, not a kernel that owns state and gates; GNOSIS should reimplement these conditions as kernel-enforced gates over durable evidence, not depend on or embed the tool.\n205-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n206:  - Task-loop dual-evidence completion (`pkg/processor/phase/task.go:TaskPhase.Run` + `HasUncompletedTasks`): loop `i=1..MaxIterations` (default 50); `ALL_TASKS_DONE` signal alone is NOT trusted — the plan file is re-parsed and if unchecked `[ ]` boxes remain the signal is discarded with a warning and the loop continues. `FAILED` signal gets bounded retries (`retryCount`, default 1) then hard error; timeout → fixed 5s `retryBackoff` re-run; exhaustion → hard error \"max iterations reached\". Invariant: completion = signal ∧ durable-artifact evidence. [VERIFIED]\n207:  - Tri-state review round contract (`pkg/config/defaults/prompts/review_first.txt` / `review_second.txt` + `pkg/processor/phase/review.go:ReviewPhase.Loop`): Path A `REVIEW_DONE` = \"this iteration found ZERO issues\" (explicitly not \"I finished fixing\"); Path B fixed-and-committed = emit NO marker → loop schedules another verification round; Path C can't-fix = `TASK_FAILED`. Loop bound `max(3, MaxIterations/10)`; also exits when git HEAD is unchanged after a round (fixed-point detection). Failure mode: max-iterations exit is soft (\"continuing...\") — unconverged review flows on. [VERIFIED]\n208:  - Stalemate detection (`pkg/processor/phase/git_state.go:stalemateState.Update`): before each Claude-eval round a `gitSnapshot{HeadHash, DiffFingerprint}` is taken; N consecutive rounds with identical snapshot (`ReviewPatience`, 0=disabled) terminates the external review early; counter resets on any change; refuses to count when head/diff evidence is missing. Invariant: no progress evidence for N rounds ⇒ STALEMATE, stop burning tokens. [VERIFIED]\n209:  - Adversarial external review loop (`pkg/processor/phase/external_review.go:runLoop`/`runIteration`): independent tool (codex read-only, or custom script) reviews → Claude verifies each finding against code and fixes or rebuts → rebuttals fed back to the external tool next round (`{{PREVIOUS_REVIEW_CONTEXT}}`). Stop set: `CODEX_REVIEW_DONE` (only legal when the external tool itself reported no issues, per `codex.txt` contract), empty external output → break, stalemate, or bound `max(3, MaxIterations/5)` → soft continue. Cross-model disagreement is thus bounded by both patience and iterations. [VERIFIED]\n210-  - Three-tier output-pattern failure taxonomy (`pkg/processor/execution_policy.go:retryPolicy.Run` + `pkg/executor/executor.go:matchPattern`): retry patterns (transient 529/502/503/504) → treated as timeout, 5s backoff, no wait gate; limit patterns → wait `wait_on_limit` then retry, else surface as error; error patterns → hard exit with help command. Retry tier is suppressed when a structured signal is present so a completed run isn't discarded. Mirrors GNOSIS `RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE`. [VERIFIED]\n211-  - Code-injected reviewer contract (`pkg/processor/prompts.go:reviewContextInstruction` + 5 specialized agents in `pkg/config/defaults/agents/`): every spawned reviewer (quality/implementation/testing/simplification/documentation, launched in parallel) gets a Go-side prepended contract — read-only (\"the parent loop owns fixing\"), no sub-delegation, evidence threshold, diff scope, `NO ISSUES FOUND` sentinel — that user prompt files cannot drop. Reviewer-judges/parent-fixes separation enforced at orchestrator level. [VERIFIED]\n212-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n213:  - **Signal ∧ evidence completion gate**: never let an agent's DONE claim close a task; the kernel re-checks a durable artifact (checkbox state, git HEAD/diff fingerprint) and downgrades signal-without-evidence to \"continue with warning\".\n214:  - **Tri-state round protocol where silence means rework**: DONE only for a zero-findings round; \"I fixed things\" must trigger another independent verification round; convergence is declared only by a clean round, never by the fixer.\n215:  - **Stalemate counter keyed on repo-state fingerprints** (HEAD + diff hash), N consecutive unchanged rounds → `STALEMATE` outcome; reset on change; never count when evidence collection failed — implement as a kernel circuit breaker.\n216-  - **Tiered pattern taxonomy with signal-suppressed retries**: retry/limit/error classes, limit-class gated on an explicit wait budget, and transient markers ignored when a structured completion signal exists.\n217:- **What to avoid:** (1) In-band substring signal detection — `detectSignal` matches `<<<RALPHEX:...>>>` anywhere in output, so a model *quoting* the marker triggers it; ralphex defends only with prompt exhortations (\"a marker is irreversible\"). GNOSIS must use out-of-band structured results. (2) Fail-open loop exhaustion — both review loops log \"max iterations reached, continuing...\" and proceed to finalize as if successful, and \"HEAD unchanged\" is read as convergence even when the reviewer may simply have failed to act; GNOSIS gates must fail closed and distinguish \"no findings\" from \"no action\".\n218-- **Scores:** determinism 6, durability 6, security 6, recovery 7, auditability 7, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 6, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 8, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 8\n219-\n220-### smithers — durable time-travel runtime/control plane for long-running coding-agent workflows (JSX workflows over SQLite/Postgres)\n221-\n222-- **Identity:** https://github.com/smithersai/smithers @ 584f479 (tag v0.35.0, 2026-08-17) | MIT, plain text, no riders (vendored `@smthrs/jj-*` binary packages carry Apache-2.0 for the Jujutsu binary) | JavaScript ESM w/ JSDoc types + type-only `.ts` sidecars, React/JSX, Effect-TS 4 beta | pnpm workspaces + Bun (Node >=22, Bun >=1.3), tsup dts-only | ~9,515 tracked files, 69 MiB pack, ~50 workspace packages\n223-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no remote telemetry found (searched posthog/segment/amplitude/sentry: only local Prometheus/OTel + opt-in stderr JSON [VERIFIED]); postinstall hooks are chmod-only on vendored jj binaries [VERIFIED]; gateway enforces loopback Host allowlist + auth token with explicit DNS-rebinding defense (`packages/server/src/serve.js`) [VERIFIED]; the MEDIUM comes from a very large supply chain (pinned `effect@4.0.0-beta.105`, electrobun, react-reconciler), release tooling that downloads jj from GitHub releases (`scripts/fetch-jj-binaries.mjs`) [VERIFIED], and its core purpose of spawning agent CLIs/sandboxes (Docker/Bubblewrap/Microsandbox) [VERIFIED design, NOT TESTED].\n224-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the mechanism designs (frame log, write-ahead tool journal, effect-boundary classification, leased/fenced destructive rewind) are exactly what a deterministic kernel that owns state and gates needs, and they are the most rigorous articulation of agent time travel seen in this survey. But the substrate (Bun + JSX re-render via react-reconciler + Effect-TS beta across ~50 packages) contradicts GNOSIS's Python/SQLite minimal-kernel constitution and would be an unauditable dependency. Steal the schemas and invariants; do not adopt the runtime.\n225-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n226-  - Frame log + content-addressed snapshots (`packages/db/src/frame-codec.js`, `packages/engine/src/engine.js:persistDriverFrame` ~L10716, `packages/time-travel/src/snapshot/captureSnapshotEffect.js`): every workflow re-render appends a row to `_smithers_frames` — every 50th a full keyframe, the rest versioned JSON-path delta ops, so reconstructing any frame is bounded to <50 applies; each frame's full state (nodes, outputs, loop state, input, plus a signal-provenance horizon) is stored as a sha256 content-addressed, refcounted snapshot row *in the same transaction as the frame insert*. Invariant: any frame is reconstructible and the snapshot, not heuristics, is the rewind source of truth. Failure mode: legacy runs without snapshots fall back to timestamp-cutoff heuristics (explicitly logged). [VERIFIED]\n227-  - Pure decision core (`packages/scheduler/src/scheduleTasks.js`, `makeWorkflowSession.js`): scheduler has no IO/DB/timers — a pure function from plan tree + task-state map (keys `nodeId::iteration`, loop scopes suffixed `@@ralphId=iteration`) to the next decision; code comments state and enforce that a resumed run replays identical activation decisions from a restored state map in descriptor order. Saga and try-catch-finally regions match failures by error code. Invariant: resume determinism = f(persisted state), engine is just the effectful shell. [VERIFIED]\n228-  - Write-ahead tool journal + effect boundary (`packages/engine/src/createToolJournalContext.js`, `packages/time-travel/src/guardEffectBoundary.js`, `assessEffectBoundary.js`): every tool/task call inserts