, `runsDueForQuotaResume`): approvals are DB rows resolved under a node lock, with approval admissions restored across resume; host shutdown parks runs as durable `paused`; rate-limit exhaustion parks as `waiting-quota` with a `resetAtMs` sweep — quota is a park state, not a failure. [VERIFIED, approvals partially read]\n232-- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) The write-ahead tool journal: persist `intended` + {sideEffect, idempotent, hasRevert, idempotencyKey} before any tool executes, making rewind/fork safety a *decidable query* over the journal instead of a guess — this slots directly into GNOSIS's evidence-before-consensus kernel. (2) Frame log = keyframe-every-N + JSON-delta rows plus content-addressed refcounted snapshots in one SQLite transaction — full run history and O(1)-bounded reconstruction at trivial cost. (3) Workflow-identity hash (entry + full import-graph sha256) pinned at run start, with resume/compensation gated on exact match and an explicit human re-bless flag — a clean Task!=session determinism contract. (4) Destructive operations as leased+fenced single transactions with a pre-mutation `in_progress` audit row and startup crash recovery; and `RATE_LIMITED` modeled as a durable park state with a reset timestamp.\n233-- **What to avoid:** The 12,000-line `engine.js` and `\"./*\"` wildcard exports that make every file in ~50 packages public API — the operational surface is enormous and filenames become unbreakable contracts. Deriving scheduler input by re-rendering JSX through a react-reconciler on a pinned *beta* dependency (`effect@4.0.0-beta.105`, Bun.Transpiler with regex fallback) puts a large nondeterminism/supply-chain surface under the determinism-critical path; GNOSIS's graph derivation must stay a small, boring pure function.\n234-- **Scores:** determinism 7, durability 9, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 8, windows 6, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 7\n235-\n236-### beads — dependency-aware distributed issue/task graph for AI agents (\"bd\"), Dolt-backed work queue with atomic claims and lease-based crash recovery\n237-\n238-- **Identity:** https://github.com/gastownhall/beads @ 309ebd4 (2026-08-19) | MIT, no riders (LICENSE read in full — standard MIT text, \"Copyright (c) 2025 Beads Contributors\") [VERIFIED] | Go 1.26.5 (~341K non-test LOC + ~456K test LOC), plus small Python MCP server (fastmcp) and npm/PS1/winget installers | Go modules + Makefile + Nix flake | 53MB working tree, 3,567 tracked files\n239-- **Risk:** LOW — for read-only pattern mining: no eval/unsafe deserialization; SQL is parameterized with hardcoded table names; telemetry is explicit opt-in (`BD_OTEL_ENABLED=true`, defaults to localhost OTLP) [VERIFIED]; Anthropic API used only for opt-in `bd compact` summarization and errors without an explicit key rather than falling back silently [VERIFIED]; Dolt sql-server listener binds 127.0.0.1 by default [VERIFIED]. If ever *run* as a tool, risk rises to MEDIUM: npm postinstall downloads release binaries from GitHub [VERIFIED], `bd init`/`bd setup claude|codex` write agent hooks/AGENTS.md into the host project [SELF-REPORTED, install code not fully read], and it spawns a local MySQL-protocol server process [VERIFIED].\n240-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — Beads solves exactly GNOSIS's persistent-task-graph problem, but as its own kernel: a Go binary owning state in Dolt (embedded via cgo, or an external `dolt sql-server` in non-cgo builds), with its own claim semantics, migrations (60+), proxy daemon, and doctor. Adopting it as a dependency would place Task state under an external runtime outside the GNOSIS Kernel/Policy Engine (violating \"Kernel authorizes states\") and import Dolt's operational weight onto a Windows workstation where SQLite-V1 is the ADR'd baseline. The mechanisms, however, are battle-tested (code comments cite specific regression IDs for every subtle choice) and map 1:1 onto SQLite reimplementation. Keep it as a secondary BENCHMARK reference for ready/claim semantics once GNOSIS's scheduler exists.\n241-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n242-  - Task/dependency schema (`internal/storage/schema/migrations/0001,0002*.sql`; `internal/types/types.go:DependencyType`): one `issues` table (status, priority, assignee, defer_until, ephemeral, await_type/await_id for gates) + one typed edge table `(issue_id, depends_on_id, type, metadata JSON)` PK'd on the pair. 19 well-known edge types but only 4 affect scheduling (`AffectsReadyWork()`: blocks, parent-child, conditional-blocks, waits-for); association/provenance edges (related, discovered-from, caused-by, authored-by…) ride the same table without touching readiness. `IsSchedulingEdge()` is the single shared cycle-set predicate, deliberately excluding waits-for because its gate clears on children, not the target, so it cannot close a cycle. Invariant: scheduler semantics live in exactly one function each. [VERIFIED]\n243-  - Ready detection via materialized `is_blocked` (`internal/storage/sqlbuild/ready.go:BuildReadyWorkWhere`; `internal/storage/issueops/blocked_state.go:markBlockedTemplateForIssues`): \"ready\" is a cheap indexed query — active status AND `is_blocked = 0` AND not pinned/ephemeral/deferred AND type not in infra excludes — because `is_blocked` is a derived column recomputed inside the same transaction as every edge/status write, by a fixpoint loop (repeat mark/unmark UPDATE passes until 0 rows change) that propagates a blocked parent down parent-child chains and evaluates waits-for fanout gates. Recompute self-assigns `updated_at = updated_at` to suppress ON UPDATE timestamps so derived state never plants per-node wall clock in synced rows (cited merge-conflict bug bd-578h9.19). A separate consistency checker (`issueops/blocked_consistency.go`) finds/repairs drift. Failure mode: drift if a write path forgets recompute — hence the checker. [VERIFIED]\n244-  - Atomic claim CAS (`internal/storage/issueops/claim.go:ClaimIssueInTx`, `ClaimReadyIssueInTx`): claim = conditional UPDATE keyed on `row_lock = <pre-read version>` where `row_lock` is rewritten by every path mutating status/assignee — one token detects all races instead of spelling-sensitive SQL predicates. Idempotent same-actor re-claim (agent retry support), pool pseudo-assignees claimable by anyone, typed `ClaimConflictError` carrying holder+status so callers never parse prose; refusal copy deliberately never names an unclaim command because batch agents pattern-matched it into steamrolling live claims (wy-yuclk). `ClaimReadyIssueInTx` computes the ready set and claims in the SAME transaction, walking past rows lost to racing claimers. [VERIFIED]\n245-  - Lease/heartbeat/reclaim for disposable workers (`internal/storage/issueops/lease.go`; migration `0055_move_leases_to_table.up.sql`): claims grant a 5-min-TTL lease in an ephemeral, node-local, non-replicated (`dolt_ignore`d) `leases` table — the claim's status/assignee transition commits to history, heartbeats mint zero commits. A dead worker stops heartbeating; `ReclaimExpiredLeasesInTx` snapshots the stale set, repeats the expiry predicate on delete so a concurrently rescued lease is skipped, reverts issues to open, and records per-issue recovery events. Invariant: durable truth (who owns what) is separated from coordination chatter (proof of liveness). [VERIFIED]\n246-  - Cycle prevention at edge insert (`internal/storage/issueops/dependencies.go:WouldCreateSchedulingCycleInTx`): recursive-CTE reachability over the combined scheduling edge set with UNION-distinct recursion (terminates on diamonds/cycles by unique node), plus a hierarchy check refusing blocks-edges between an issue and its own ancestor/descendant. Cycles are refused before they exist rather than detected after. [VERIFIED]\n247-  - Fanout gates & workflow templates (`types.go:WaitsForMeta`, `cmd/bd/gate.go`, `internal/molecules`): waits-for edges carry JSON gate metadata (all-children / any-children, spawner ID) so a coordinator bead unblocks when dynamically spawned children finish; standalone gate beads await human/timer/gh:run/gh:pr/bead conditions; \"molecules\" are read-only JSONL issue templates instantiated into subgraphs. [VERIFIED schema/types; gate evaluation loop only partially read — INFERRED it polls via `bd gate check`]\n248-- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) Single-table typed edge model where only a tiny enumerated subset participates in scheduling, with one shared predicate function per semantic set (ready-affecting, cycle-set) so a new edge type is a one-line change; (2) materialized `is_blocked` maintained by in-transaction fixpoint recompute + an independent drift checker as a repairable, testable invariant — makes ready-detection O(indexed scan) instead of graph walk per poll; (3) claim-as-CAS on a row-version token rewritten by every ownership mutation, idempotent for same-actor retries, returning typed conflict data (holder, status) — never prose — and claiming inside the same transaction that computed readiness; (4) two-plane state split: history-worthy transitions in the durable/versioned store, leases+heartbeats in an ephemeral table that never enters history, with reclaim-by-TTL as the crash-recovery path for disposable workers.\n249-- **What to avoid:** Two parallel storage stacks (\"classic\" and \"domain\") whose ready semantics are held identical only by a \"Seam A parity\" test suite — semantics defined twice is a standing drift risk they visibly pay for; and the Dolt-scale machinery (embedded-vs-server cgo split, sql-server autostart, dbproxy daemon, 60+ guarded migrations, doctor repair surface) — the claim scan is also deliberately unbounded over the ready set (noted in claim.go as a deferred fix), fine for hundreds of tasks, a footgun at scale.\n250-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 9, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 8, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\n251-\n252-### agent-governance-toolkit — Microsoft's agent governance monorepo whose core is ACS: a stateless, deterministic, fail-closed policy decision engine (Rust) hooked into agent loops at 8 intervention points\n253-\n254-- **Identity:** https://github.com/microsoft/agent-governance-toolkit @ 7d0cef5 (2026-08-12, shallow clone depth 1) | MIT (Microsoft); vendored-attribution `policy-engine/LICENSE.acs` is also plain MIT \"(c) 2026 responsibleai\" — no unusual riders in either [VERIFIED] | Python ~1.9k files, TS/JS ~440, C# 151, Rust 142 (the decision core), Rego 122, Go 57 | Cargo workspace + maturin/PyO3, napi-rs, .NET P/Invoke, npm, Python packaging | ~80MB, 4787 tracked files\n255-- **Risk:** LOW (for read-only pattern mining) — no npm/pip install hooks found in the plugin or checked packages [VERIFIED]; OPA runs as external subprocess with timeout and Windows-aware binary name (`core/src/opa.rs`) [VERIFIED]; runtime network is explicit and gated: HTTPS-only manifest `extends`/bundle fetch with SHA-256 pin, size/redirect/timeout limits, fail-closed on mismatch (`manifest.rs:290-320`) [VERIFIED]; telemetry is redaction-safe by construction (content fields omitted, evidence pointer keys only, never URLs) [VERIFIED]; MCP exposure is a local stdio server with two read-only tools (`agent-governance-claude-code/server/agt-mcp.mjs`) [VERIFIED]; Dockerfile is a non-root dev container that curls toolchains at build time only [VERIFIED]. Caveats: unpinned URL `extends` is permitted (\"trusted because the host chose it\") and LLM-annotator `api_key` may be inlined in the manifest rather than env [VERIFIED].\n256-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — The ACS decision model (host-as-PEP builds a complete JSON snapshot; stateless PDP returns one of allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform; every runtime failure collapses to deny with a reserved reason) is exactly the shape GNOSIS's Kernel/Policy Engine needs, and it is the best-evidenced fail-closed design in the survey (Quint formal model + cross-SDK conformance parity). But GNOSIS's kernel is Python/SQLite/Windows-native and should not take a Rust+maturin+OPA dependency for V1; reimplement the contract (points, verdict enum, reserved reasons, action-identity hashing, limits) in the kernel, and revisit the PyO3 SDK as a dependency candidate only if policy complexity later demands Rego/Cedar.\n257-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n258-  - Fail-closed PDP (`policy-engine/core/src/runtime.rs:evaluate_intervention_point`, `verdict.rs:Verdict::runtime_error`, `error.rs`): every failure — invalid manifest, missing path, unknown tool, annotator error, policy dispatcher error or panic (caught via `catch_unwind`), oversized/deep output, invalid verdict shape — maps to `Decision::Deny` with a reserved `runtime_error:<code>` reason; policies/annotators are forbidden from emitting that prefix (`limits.rs:reject_reserved_reason`), so a deny-by-error is always distinguishable from a policy deny. Invariant: no error path ever permits. Formally stated as `RuntimeErrorsDeny` and `ExplicitAllowRequired` in the Quint model (`tests/formal/acs_mediation.qnt`). [VERIFIED]\n259-  - Eight closed intervention points + unconfigured-point-denies (`intervention_point.rs`; `agent-os/src/agent_os/integrations/_native_adapter_runtime.py:POINT_NOT_CONFIGURED`): agent_startup/input/pre_model_call/post_model_call/pre_tool_call/post_tool_call/output/agent_shutdown as a closed enum; a request naming a point the manifest omits returns `runtime_error:intervention_point_unknown` and does NOT permit — the code comment explicitly argues a missing binding is a configuration gap, not consent, and a `post_*` block must stop already-produced output from propagating. [VERIFIED]\n260-  - Action identity & approval binding (`policy_input.rs:action_identity`, `sdk/rust/src/host/approval.rs`, `error.rs:ApprovalActionMismatch`): identity = `sha256:` over key-sorted canonical JSON of the full policy input; `input_identity` vs `enforced_identity` (recomputed over the transformed value); an `escalate` verdict routes through a host `ApprovalResolver` whose resolution is bound to the exact action hash — mismatch fails closed. Anti-TOCTOU between \"what was approved\" and \"what runs\". [VERIFIED]\n261-  - Transform confinement (`verdict.rs:Transform::from_value`, `runtime.rs` D1.1 block): the only value-mutating decision is `transform`; its path must root at `$policy_target` (`TransformTargetForbidden` otherwise), the rewritten snapshot is re-validated against resource limits before surfacing, and adapters gate on `permits_unchanged`/`applies_to` so an unapplicable transform is treated as a deny rather than silently running the original (the \"redaction that silently doesn't redact\" failure mode is designed out). [VERIFIED]\n262-  - Least privilege via tool catalog + IFC label lattice (`tool_projection.rs`, `policy/lib/agt_ifc.rego`): a tool name resolved from the snapshot that is not declared in the manifest catalog → `ToolUnknown` → deny (deny-by-default for undeclared tools); declared tools carry `clearance`/`security_labels` projected into policy input; a stock Rego lattice (public<internal<confidential<secret) enforces no-write-down (`sink clearance must dominate max source label sensitivity`) with label propagation via `result_labels` — the host tracks provenance, the engine stays stateless. [VERIFIED]\n263-  - Evidence + conformance + resource limits (`verdict.rs:Evidence`, `tests/conformance/`, `tests/formal/`, `limits.rs`): verdicts may carry a 4KiB-bounded evidence object (artefact content-address + verification pointers) propagated to telemetry as keys-not-URLs; cross-SDK parity suites include `fail_closed_error_parity.json`; hard limits on snapshot bytes, JSON depth, annotator/policy output size, extends depth, and URL fetches. Tests themselves NOT TESTED (protocol forbids execution); their presence and content VERIFIED by reading.\n264-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n265-  1. **The snapshot→verdict contract as the Kernel's policy gate:** agents/adapters build a complete JSON snapshot per intervention point; a pure, stateless kernel function returns exactly one of `allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform`, and *every* internal failure returns deny with a reserved `runtime_error:*` reason namespace that policies may not use — this cleanly implements \"agents propose, Kernel authorizes\" and rule 29 (never suppress gates).\n266-  2. **Action-identity hashing for approvals:** sha256 over canonical (key-sorted) JSON of the evaluated action, with separate input/enforced identities, and approval resolutions bound to that hash so a NEEDS_HUMAN escalation can never authorize a different action than the one evaluated.\n267-  3. **Unconfigured-point-denies + undeclared-tool-denies:** treat a missing policy binding or an unregistered tool as a configuration gap that fails closed, never as implicit allow — directly implements GNOSIS rule 13 (deny-by-default).\n268-  4. **Formal invariants + parity fixtures as evidence:** a tiny machine-checked model (their 7 Quint invariants like RuntimeErrorsDeny) plus JSON conformance fixtures runnable against the kernel implementation — cheap to port to Python/pytest and exactly the \"evidence before consensus\" artifact GNOSIS milestones need.\n269-- **What to avoid:** (1) Toolkit sprawl — the monorepo surrounds a ~15k-line disciplined Rust core with ~19k lines of loosely-coupled Python \"agent-os\" modules (mcp_security, prompt_injection, credential_vault, sandbox, …) of much more variable rigor; steal the core contract, not the kitchen sink. (2) Regex-heuristic enforcement in the Claude Code plugin (`lib/policy.mjs`): command-pattern matching with hardcoded bypass lists (`SAFE_CLEANUP_TARGETS`, env-template regexes) and path-field name sniffing is easily evaded and drifts from the declarative manifest model — GNOSIS gates must key on structured tool args validated by the kernel, not string pattern-matching, and never hardcode bypass allowlists inside the enforcement path. Also note inline `api_key` in manifests is permitted — GNOSIS should require env/secret-store only.\n270-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 5, security 9, recovery 5, auditability 8, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 8, op-simplicity 4, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 9\n271-\n272-### grit — AST-symbol-level lock manager coordinating parallel AI agents over git worktrees\n273-\n274-- **Identity:** https://github.com/rtk-ai/grit @ 0f3c9d0 (2026-06-14) | Apache-2.0, no riders (copyright \"2025-2026 RTK AI\") [VERIFIED, read LICENSE] | Rust 2021 (~6.7k LOC src + shell bench scripts) | Cargo; deps: rusqlite (bundled SQLite), tree-sitter x13 grammars, aws-sdk-s3, azure_storage_blobs, tokio | ~1.8 MB worktree, 450 KB pack; no build.rs, no install hooks [VERIFIED]\n275-- **Risk:** LOW — no telemetry, no remote code fetch, no listeners beyond a local Unix socket at `.grit/room.sock`; subprocesses are only `git`/`gh`/`kill` [VERIFIED]. Caveats: Azure access key stored plaintext in `.grit/config.json` (gitignored) [VERIFIED, `src/config.rs` + `cli/mod.rs:1494`]; `rm -rf` only in benchmark scripts on their own temp dirs [VERIFIED]. Repo ships its own CLAUDE.md pushing vendor tools (rtk/icm) — instruction-bearing content, ignore when analyzing [VERIFIED].\n276-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the symbol-lease table (atomic check-then-set, TTL, read/write modes, intent metadata, FIFO queue) is exactly the \"semantic ownership\" layer GNOSIS Rule 15 calls for, and it is small enough (~1.5k LOC of logic) to rebuild inside the Kernel where GNOSIS owns state and gates. As a dependency it is disqualified: it does not compile on native Windows (`std::os::unix::net` un-gated in `src/room/mod.rs:3`, `kill -0` for lock liveness in `src/git/mod.rs:312`) [VERIFIED], and its locks are advisory — nothing verifies an agent only changed claimed symbols, which GNOSIS must enforce as a merge gate.\n277-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n278-  - Atomic symbol lease (`src/db/sqlite_store.rs:SqliteLockStore::try_lock`): `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction makes the expire→check→insert sequence atomic across processes; lazy TTL expiry deletes stale locks in the same transaction; compatibility matrix: write excludes all, reads share, read blocks write; same-agent re-claim updates intent/TTL idempotently. Invariant: at most one live write lock per symbol, proven by a regression test racing 16 separate connections. Failure mode: TTL expiry is not fenced — a slow agent's lease can lapse, a second agent claims the symbol, and the first can still merge later. [VERIFIED]\n279-  - Claim/grant protocol with all-or-nothing wait (`src/cli/mod.rs:cmd_claim`): ownership is *declared* per AST symbol id (`file::name`) with agent, intent, mode, TTL; on `--wait`, granted locks are released before each backoff sleep so an agent never holds partial claims while blocked (hold-and-wait avoidance); `--queue` enqueues FIFO with position-preserving re-enqueue (`db/mod.rs:enqueue`, rowid tiebreak). Failure modes: queue promotion hardcodes 600s TTL and skips worktree creation (acknowledged TODO at `cli/mod.rs:693`). [VERIFIED]\n280-  - Dependency-aware conflict semantics (`src/db/mod.rs:get_transitive_deps` + `cmd_claim --with-deps`): recursive CTE over a tree-sitter-extracted call graph; claiming `login` write-locks it and read-locks all transitive callees, so no other agent can rewrite an interface you depend on — conflict prevention *beyond* textual merge, at the semantic-dependency level. Invariant: a symbol under read lock cannot be write-claimed. Failure mode: dep graph is only rebuilt at `grit init`, so it goes stale as agents edit. [VERIFIED]\n281-  - Worktree lifecycle + serialized integration (`src/git/mod.rs:merge_worktree`): per-agent worktree `.grit/worktrees/<agent>` on branch `agent/<id>`; done = auto-commit, rebase onto current branch *inside the worktree*, then `merge --no-ff` in main, all under a PID-liveness-checked file spinlock (never steals from a live holder); refuses to merge into a dirty main tree and preserves branch+worktree on any failure so work is recoverable. [VERIFIED]\n282-  - Enforcement gap (negative finding): `cmd_done` (`src/cli/mod.rs:890`) commits `git add -A` — everything the agent touched, claimed or not — and never diffs changes against held locks (the stored per-symbol `hash` is written at init and never re-checked); locks are released and queued agents promoted even when the merge failed. Ownership is a convention agents must follow, not a gate. [VERIFIED — absence confirmed by reading `cmd_done` and grepping hash usage]\n283-  - Distributed backend fail-closed discipline (`src/db/s3_store.rs:put_lock_if_absent`): conditional PUT `If-None-Match:*`; treats only 501/NotImplemented as \"fallback allowed\" and refuses to degrade to non-atomic locking on any other error, explicitly to avoid granting two agents the same write lock. Good policy prose in code. [VERIFIED]\n284-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n285-  - A Kernel-owned `symbol_leases` SQLite table using exactly grit's shape — `(symbol_id, agent_id, intent, mode, locked_at, ttl_seconds)` PK `(symbol_id, agent_id)`, `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` check-then-set, lazy expiry in the same transaction, FK to a symbols registry so unindexed symbols are unclaimable.\n286-  - Read/write lease modes plus `--with-deps` transitive read-locking of callees (recursive CTE) as the semantic-ownership answer to Rule 15/16 — writers get exclusivity, readers pin interfaces they depend on.\n287-  - Upgrade advisory to enforced: at the GNOSIS merge gate, diff the agent branch, map hunks back to AST symbols, and fail `FAIL_POLICY` if changed-symbols ⊄ claimed-symbols — the check grit conspicuously lacks.\n288-  - The all-or-nothing claim retry (release partial grants before sleeping) and position-preserving re-enqueue (ON CONFLICT UPDATE, not INSERT OR REPLACE) as deadlock/starvation hygiene; add fencing tokens (lease epoch checked at merge time) to close grit's TTL-expiry hole.\n289-- **What to avoid:** (1) Symbol identity as bare `file::name` — two same-named methods in different classes in one file collide into one lock row (last upsert wins), and renames silently change identity; GNOSIS needs scope-qualified, hash-anchored symbol ids. (2) Releasing locks and promoting the queue even when the merge failed (`cmd_done`), which hands a contested symbol to the next agent while the previous agent's unmerged changes sit in a leftover branch — in GNOSIS the Kernel must keep the lease bound to the unresolved integration state.\n290-- **Scores:** determinism 7, durability 6, security 6, recovery 7, auditability 5, modularity 8, testability 8, windows 2, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 6, gnosis-fit 7\n291-\n292-### opentraces — local-first agent-trace capture: schema + append-only Git event log + evidence-graded attribution\n293-\n294-- **Identity:** https://github.com/jayfarei/opentraces @ 2ca75f7 (2026-08-19) | MIT with verbatim rider: *\"NOTE: Data contributed through the opentraces CLI to HuggingFace Hub is licensed under CC-BY-4.0 by default, unless the contributor specifies a different license for their personal dataset repository.\"* | Python 3.10+ (core), TypeScript (pi-extension, web) | hatchling + pip/uv (uv.lock), Node for web | ~2,118 files, 36.6 MiB pack, 640 test files, ~18.7k LOC in trails+otlp alone\n295-- **Risk:** LOW — no postinstall hooks, no eval/exec/os.system in `src/` (VERIFIED grep), no telemetry/analytics egress (VERIFIED grep); OTLP listener binds 127.0.0.1:4318 by default and warns on non-loopback (VERIFIED `capture/otlp/receiver.py`); HF publishing is opt-in behind one conservative egress predicate (VERIFIED `core/egress_clearance.py`, `unknown` → withheld). Residual notes: `pickle` accelerator caches under the Git common dir (`trails/event_index.py:328 pickle.loads`) — local-trust deserialization surface; installers rewrite Claude Code/Codex `settings.json` hooks, but only via explicit `opentraces setup <agent>` (VERIFIED `capture/claude_code/install.py`, no import-time side effects seen). [Static audit only; nothing executed — runtime behavior NOT TESTED]\n296-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the event-envelope, evidence-tier, and honesty-vocabulary patterns are precisely what GNOSIS's kernel-owned state/evidence layer needs, but the codebase is VERIFIED POSIX-only (`import fcntl` at module top of `core/state.py:19` and `core/bucket_store.py:25`; `os.fork()` at `cli/capture_otlp.py:538`; tmux test harness), operationally enormous (buckets/trails/capsules/datasets/workflows), and aimed at HF dataset publishing GNOSIS doesn't need. Reimplement the envelope + anchoring vocabulary natively in the GNOSIS kernel; do not depend on it.\n297-- **Signature mechanisms:**\n298-  - Hash-chained append-only event log in Git refs (`src/opentraces/core/trails/models.py:TrailEvent,finalize_event` + `event_log.py:_update_event_log_ref`): every event is content-addressed (`event_id = sha256(canonical_json(material))` where material *includes* `event_sequence` + `previous_event_id`, making replay order tamper-evident) and stamped with `SCHEMA_VERSION`/`SECURITY_VERSION`/`ATTRIBUTION_VERSION`; appends land under `refs/opentraces/local/events/v1` via compare-and-swap `git update-ref <ref> <new> <expected-old>` with race detection and max 5 retries. Invariant: single total order, tamper-evident, survives any agent, no server. Failure modes: log growth (mitigated by snapshot+delta reads), pickle cache staleness (guarded by `_EVENT_CACHE_FORMAT`). [VERIFIED]\n299-  - Reconstructable-task schema (`packages/opentraces-schema/src/opentraces_schema/models.py:TraceRecord,Step,Patch,GitAnchor`): one JSONL line per session; step-based TAO loop with `ToolCall`→`Observation` joined by `source_call_id` (dangling calls get explicit `error='no_result'`), sub-agent hierarchy via `parent_step`/`subagent_trajectory_ref`, deduplicated `system_prompts` keyed by hash, `content_hash` excluding `trace_id` for dedup, `lifecycle: provisional→final`, and `generation_index` replacement-snapshot semantics (later generations are NOT stitchable supersets). `patches[]` is the authoritative output spine; `outcome.commit_sha`/`git_links` are derived projections. [VERIFIED]\n300-  - Evidence-graded trace→commit anchoring (`models.py:GitAnchor` + `core/trails/sync.py`, `capture/git/post_commit.py`): each Edit/Write is one content-addressed trace patch; a post-commit hook matches it into Git and records a 10-value `evidence_tier` (`exact_blob_hash` → `structural_symbol` → `orphan`) plus a 7-value `evidence_firmness` (`firm_observed` → `model_inferred`), with `superseded_by` sha chains for rebase/squash/amend. Invariant: attribution is never a boolean — every claim carries its evidence grade. [schema/vocab VERIFIED; hook runtime behavior INFERRED from code+docs, NOT TESTED]\n301-  - Context Tree substrate (`core/context_tree/models.py:ContextLayer,ContextNode` + `contract.py`): content-addressed layers of *what the model actually saw* (system / messages / tool_registry / runtime_state), each labeled `completeness: full|approximated|stub` and `capture_method: live_capture|transcript_reconstruction|proxy|otel|hardcoded_template`; `Step.context_node_id` joins record→context in one hop. Invariant: reconstruction fidelity is explicit data; \"presence never raises trust tier\". [VERIFIED]\n302-  - OTel ingestion, not emission (`capture/otlp/receiver.py`, `mapper.py`): stdlib `ThreadingHTTPServer` OTLP/HTTP+JSON receiver (loopback:4318, JSON-only, protobuf rejected with a hint); pure-function mapper reads `gen_ai.*` semconv + `claude_code.*` attributes; raw request/response bodies paired per LLM request by `(prompt.id, event.sequence)` because request bodies carry no `request_id`; bypass-safe (receiver down ⇒ telemetry dropped, agent never blocked). Export side is lossy ATIF v1.6 + Agent Trace serializers (`publish/atif.py` docstring enumerates exactly what is dropped). [VERIFIED]\n303-  - Single egress predicate (`core/egress_clearance.py:clearance_state`): one leaf-module three-way gate (`cleared`/`not_cleared`/`unknown`) behind all three egress doors (bucket sync, dataset publish, capsule share); absence of recorded clearance is never coerced to \"safe\". [VERIFIED]\n304-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\n305-  - Event envelope pattern: content-addressed event id computed over canonical JSON that *includes* sequence + previous-event pointer, with schema/security/attribution version stamps on every event — gives the GNOSIS kernel a tamper-evident, replayable log with pure stdlib + Git.\n306-  - CAS append to a hidden Git ref (`update-ref` with expected-old-value, bounded retry) as the durable, serverless event-log write primitive — directly implements GNOSIS rule 14 (Git as architecture) with multi-writer safety.\n307-  - Closed evidence vocabularies instead of booleans: grade every claim (evidence_tier × firmness), and label every reconstruction with `completeness` + `capture_method` — this operationalizes EVIDENCE BEFORE CONSENSUS and \"Memory != Truth\" as schema, not policy prose.\n308-  - Three-substrate task reconstruction: curated record (what happened) + append-only event trail (full detail, by patch_id) + context tree (what the model saw), cross-referenced by content hashes — a task is reconstructable iff all three join.\n309-- **What to avoid:** (1) POSIX-only primitives at module import time (`fcntl`, `os.fork`) — GNOSIS is Windows-native; use `msvcrt`/portalocker-style locks and threads/processes, never fork. (2) Pickle accelerator caches and unbounded per-item event fan-out — their anchor-search events needed three schema versions (v1→v2→v3, per `trails/contract.py` comments describing a ~26GB blowup) to become bounded; design summary events O(1) from day one and persist caches as JSON/SQLite.\n310-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 8, security 7, recovery 7, auditability 10, modularity 7, testability 8, windows 2, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 8\n311-\n312-### nono — zero-setup OS-level least-privilege sandbox for running AI agents\n313-\n314-- **Identity:** https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono @ bc1406e | Apache-2.0 (standard text, copyright \"Always Further, Inc\"; no riders — VERIFIED) | Rust (+C bindings)\n315-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — the sandbox design is sound (kernel-enforced, restrictions irreversible once applied), but adoption surface includes `curl | sh` install and a remote profile registry (`registry.nono.sh`) that distributes filesystem/network policy as pullable packages; on this Windows workstation it only works under WSL2.\n316-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — strongest candidate in the corpus for the \"run untrusted agent with least privilege\" primitive, pending Dependency Admission and WSL2 validation.\n317-- **Key mechanism:** `crates/nono/src/sandbox/mod.rs` + `linux.rs` (`PreparedLandlockSandbox::apply_raw`, `install_seccomp_notify`) — on Linux it builds a Landlock LSM ruleset via raw `SYS_landlock_create_ruleset`/`landlock_add_rule`/`landlock_restrict_self` syscalls plus seccomp-notify filters for network mediation (a supervisor arbitrates `connect`/`sendto`/`openat2` via `SeccompNotif`); on macOS it uses Seatbelt with dynamic extension issuance (`extension_issue_file`). No container/VM, so zero startup latency. [VERIFIED — read the syscall-level implementation; \"zero latency\" marketing claim SELF-REPORTED]\n318-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Kernel-enforced deny-by-default (Landlock + seccomp-notify supervisor) is the right enforcement floor beneath GNOSIS's policy layer — hooks alone are advisory, this is not.\n319-\n320-### agentjail — pre-execution policy guardrail (allow/deny/ask) for coding-agent tool calls\n321-\n322-- **Identity:** https://github.com/LuD1161/agentjail @ 0c112c2 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"agentjail contributors\"; NOTICE only credits Gryph pricing data; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go (+Rego)\n323-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — very large trust surface for a guardrail: MITM network tunnel, credential broker/secrets vault, persistent daemon, PATH shims, self-update; macOS/Linux only; single primary maintainer despite fast release cadence.\n324-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — reimplement the hook→policy-daemon→typed-verdict pattern inside the GNOSIS Kernel/Policy Engine rather than adopting the whole stack.\n325-- **Key mechanism:** `internal/policyeval/evaluator.go` (`evaluator.Eval`) + `agentpolicy/policies/default.rego` — every agent PreToolUse hook call is normalized (command parsed into `program`/`flags`/`paths_resolved` intents via `internal/commandintent`), then evaluated against OPA Rego rules that return `{\"action\": \"deny\"|\"ask\"|\"allow\", \"rule_id\": ...}` with deny rules like `no-recursive-delete-of-protected-paths` firing on structured argv, not string matching. [VERIFIED — read evaluator and Rego; the \"~8 ms median\" latency claim is SELF-REPORTED]\n326-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Policy decisions over *parsed command intent* (program + flags + resolved paths) with explicit allow/ask/deny + rule_id provenance is exactly the shape GNOSIS's deny-by-default sensitive-action gate should take.\n327-\n328-### sandbox-probe — empirical measurement of a sandbox boundary from the inside\n329-\n330-- **Identity:** https://github.com/controlplaneio/sandbox-probe @ c8a31f2 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"control-plane.io\"; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go (59 files, single static binary)\n331-- **Risk:** LOW for the code itself (read-and-report only, reputable vendor); operational note: its reports enumerate readable secrets paths, open sockets, and reachable hosts, so report files are themselves sensitive.\n332-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — small, auditable verification tool; use it to prove any GNOSIS sandbox actually blocks what the policy claims (baseline-vs-sandbox diff), and to detect boundary regressions per release.\n333-- **Key mechanism:** `pkg/tasks/tasks.go` (`taskRegistry`, `taskSetRegistry`, `Task` interface) — a deterministic, sequential registry of probe tasks (`baseline_path_task`, `baseline_network_task`, `baseline_socket_task`, etc.), each emitting `Finding` objects with stable `finding_type` strings (`sensitive_readable_paths`, `unix_socket_detection`, `sandbox_detection`, ...) into a JSON report; run once unconfined, once inside the sandbox, and the diff *is* the measured boundary. [VERIFIED — read the registry code; task list matches README]\n334-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** \"Sandbox boundary = measured diff between host baseline and in-sandbox report\" turns GNOSIS's rule 24/13 assumptions into executable evidence — adopt as a mandatory pre-autonomy smoke test.\n335-\n336-### reprise — deterministic record/replay and fork-from-step for agent runs\n337-\n338-- **Identity:** https://github.com/itsshreyasbhardwaj-design/reprise @ fbd6284 | MIT (standard 2026 Shreyas Bhardwaj; no riders — VERIFIED) | Python\n339-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — v0.1.0, single individual maintainer, installed from Git not PyPI; recordings capture full LLM/tool payloads (secret-bearing) into portable SQLite files; the mechanism itself is offline and benign.\n340-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the replay engine is ~50 lines of clear logic; GNOSIS should own this pattern inside its evidence/trace layer rather than depend on a pre-1.0 solo project.\n341-- **Key mechanism:** `src/reprise/replay.py` (`Replayer.respond`) + `blobs.py` (`blob_hash`, `canonical_bytes`) — requests are canonicalized to JSON and content-hashed; replay matching is occurrence-aware (the Nth identical request gets the Nth recorded response), served from a content-addressed, de-duplicated blob store in one SQLite `.reprise` file; strict mode raises `ReplayMiss` on divergence, and `fork(at=N)` replays steps <N then goes live, with `diff --gate` giving a CI exit code on divergence. [VERIFIED — read Replayer and blob hashing; \"byte-for-byte\" claim consistent with code but end-to-end determinism SELF-REPORTED]\n342-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Content-hash, occurrence-aware response serving from a single SQLite trace is the cheapest credible way to make GNOSIS agent failures reproducible and to turn golden runs into regression fixtures.\n343-\n344-### agent-capsule — tamper-evident, signed hash-chain receipts of agent sessions\n345-\n346-- **Identity:** https://github.com/quantumpipes/agent-capsule @ 2043076 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"Quantum Pipes Technologies, LLC\"; NOTICE explicitly invites independent reimplementation of the wire format — VERIFIED) | Python (PyNaCl only) + JS browser verifier\n347-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code is small and clean, but: adapters are deliberately fail-open (integrity gaps are silent by design), the Ed25519 key lives on the same machine as the agent (a host-level attacker can re-sign a rewritten chain), and the README's \"paste this into your agent to fetch INSTALL.md and execute it\" onboarding is precisely the remote-instruction pattern GNOSIS forbids.\n348-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — adopt the six-section capsule + hash-chain wire format (NOTICE encourages interop reimplementation) inside GNOSIS's proof-packet/evidence layer, keyed under Kernel authority instead of a local fail-open hook.\n349-- **Key mechanism:** `src/agent_capsule/core/seal.py` (`compute_hash`, `Seal`) + `core/chain.py` (`CapsuleChain.seal_and_store`/`verify`) + `core/sealing.py` (`seal_specs`) — each action becomes a capsule answering six questions (trigger, context, reasoning, authority, execution, outcome), serialized to canonical JSON, hashed with SHA3-256, Ed25519-signed over the UTF-8 hash hex string, and linked to the previous capsule's hash; verification walks the chain and reports the exact `seq` where any byte was altered. Notably it records an `authority_type` (`autonomous`/`policy`/`human_approved`) derived from the agent's permission mode. [VERIFIED — read seal/chain/sealing code]\n350-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Hash-linked, signed capsules with an explicit per-action authority field give GNOSIS \"evidence before consensus\" a cryptographic backbone — but only if sealing is fail-closed and the key is held by the Kernel, not the agent host.\n351-\n352-### catacomb — statistical regression gate over recorded, secret-redacted agent-run evidence\n353-\n354-- **Identity:** https://github.com/realkarych/catacomb @ 91bc99c | Apache-2.0 (verbatim template — appendix copyright line left as literal `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]`; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go\n355-- **Risk:** LOW — plain local files, no daemon/network, CI-tested with coverage; operational caution only: `catacomb bench` spends real Claude Code money, and redaction is explicitly best-effort.\n356-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — directly implements GNOSIS rule 21 (\"no promotion without baseline/regression\") for Claude Code agents; evaluate via Dependency Admission before wiring into the promotion gate.\n357-- **Key mechanism:** `evidence/evidence.go` (`Meta`, env stamps, zstd-compressed run records) + `evidence/artifacts.go` (`CaptureArtifacts` routes every text artifact through `redactArtifactBytes`) + `redact/redact.go` (vendor-specific regexes — AWS/GitHub/OpenAI/Slack/JWT — plus Shannon-entropy-gated detection and fixed-point placeholders that never re-match) — each benchmark rep is stored as a self-describing evidence directory (run metadata, model/version/resource stamps, redacted transcript and artifacts), and `catacomb regress` compares baseline vs candidate groups with small-sample statistics, mapping the verdict to a CI exit code; `calibrate` A/A-splits a variant against itself to audit the gate's own sensitivity. [VERIFIED — read evidence and redact packages; statistical-method soundness SELF-REPORTED]\n358-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Redact-before-persist evidence directories plus a self-auditing statistical gate (A/A calibration) is the reference design for GNOSIS's promotion/regression gate — steal the calibration idea even if the tool isn't adopted.\n359-\n360----\n361-**Corpus notes:** All six LICENSE files were read head, tail, and grep-scanned mid-body for riders (commercial restrictions, revocation, use-limits): none found; the restrictive-rider repo previously seen in this corpus is not among these six. Catacomb's LICENSE retains unfilled Apache boilerplate `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]` — harmless but worth flagging in Dependency Admission. Nothing was executed, built, or modified; all inspection was Read/Grep/`git` metadata read-only.\n362-\n363-### spec-kit — GitHub's spec-driven development toolkit: phase-gated command templates that make the spec the source artifact and code the generated output\n364-\n365-- **Identity:** https://github.com/github/spec-kit @ 7eee05d (2026-08-19) | MIT (standard 21-line text, no riders — VERIFIED) | Python (specify_cli) + Markdown templates\n366-- **Risk:** LOW — official GitHub repo, MIT; the CLI only scaffolds templates into a project. Only caution: `templates/commands/*.md` support an `.specify/extensions.yml` hook mechanism that can inject third-party pre/post prompts into each phase, so any adopted template must be audited for hook wiring. [VERIFIED]\n367-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — GNOSIS already owns `gnosis-spec/`; reimplement the phase-gated pipeline (specify → clarify → plan → tasks → analyze → implement, each with structured handoffs) as Kernel task states rather than depending on the CLI.\n368-- **Key mechanism:** `spec-driven.md` (methodology) + `templates/commands/` (specify.md, clarify.md, plan.md, tasks.md, analyze.md, checklist.md, implement.md, converge.md). Each command template is a prompt contract with YAML frontmatter declaring `handoffs` to the next-phase agent (e.g. specify → speckit.plan / speckit.clarify), turning the SDLC into an explicit state machine of artifact-producing phases; `templates/*-template.md` (constitution, spec, plan, tasks, checklist) define the durable artifacts each phase must emit. The methodology doc states the inversion explicitly: \"Specifications don't serve code—code serves specifications,\" with continuous consistency validation instead of one-time gates. [VERIFIED]\n369-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Encode spec→plan→tasks as first-class Kernel-gated states with typed handoffs and per-phase artifact templates, so no Task reaches implementation without a versioned spec artifact upstream.\n370-\n371-### superpowers — Composable skill-pack methodology that force-triggers process skills (brainstorm → plan → subagent-driven TDD) before an agent writes code\n372-\n373-- **Identity:** https://github.com/obra/superpowers @ b36e082 (2026-08-12) | MIT (standard, no riders — VERIFIED) | Markdown skills + JS/shell hooks\n374-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — a `SessionStart` hook (`hooks/hooks.json` → `run-hook.cmd session-start`) executes code on every session start/clear/compact, and default-on telemetry loads a remote logo carrying the Superpowers version from the vendor's site (opt-out via `SUPERPOWERS_DISABLE_TELEMETRY`; README §\"Visual companion telemetry\" — VERIFIED). No project data is sent per their claim [SELF-REPORTED]. Also a commercial arm (Prime Radiant).\n375-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — do not install as a plugin dependency; port the skill-gating discipline and 2-3 specific skills' structure into GNOSIS-native skills without the hook/telemetry surface.\n376-- **Key mechanism:** `skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md` is a meta-skill enforcing \"invoke relevant skills BEFORE any response or action,\" including a \"Red Flags\" rationalization table that pre-empts skill-skipping; the 14 skills (`brainstorming`, `writing-plans`, `subagent-driven-development`, `test-driven-development`, `systematic-debugging`, `verification-before-completion`, `using-git-worktrees`, ...) compose into a full methodology where plans are written \"for an enthusiastic junior engineer with poor taste... and an